{"total_count":26,"incomplete_results":false,"items":[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/3605","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/3605/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/3605/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/3605/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/3605","id":4646909832,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABFPo3iA","number":3605,"title":"[RFC] FlashInfer Release-Quality Action Plan","user":{"login":"YangXu1990uiuc","id":38851819,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM4ODUxODE5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/38851819?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc","html_url":"https://github.com/YangXu1990uiuc","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/YangXu1990uiuc/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":10086439813,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACWTLbhQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/op:%20moe","name":"op: moe","color":"9db476","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":14,"created_at":"2026-06-12T06:26:58Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T11:03:04Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"# FlashInfer Release-Quality Action Plan\n\n*Owner: Yang Xu · written for community review — feedback welcome.*\n\n> **Status update 2026-09-06.** Both harnesses are merged and gate CI by default; the MoE fuzzer has\n> since caught two more real bugs; Stream 0 landed a per-unit timeout runner. Sampling and Attention\n> are still unstarted (contributors wanted — see §3); Norm/RoPE is being picked up by @0z5a. Changes\n> from the June text are marked *(updated)*. Running tally of harness-found bugs added at the end of §3.\n\n## TL;DR\n\nRecent release-quality gaps cluster in low-precision and MoE, and reached users mainly because the\ntest signal was too weak to gate on and architecture coverage was thin. The plan: port one proven\ntest kit — 7 invariant oracles + an authoritative reference + adversarial inputs — op by op, run it\non a trustworthy, gating CI signal, and back it with complementary tiers (perf, compute-sanitizer,\nframework-integration). Two harnesses (MoE, scaled GEMM) are already built and each found real bugs\nsoon after landing; the rest are net-new. Then re-audit what still escapes and repeat the loop.\n\n## Why this doc exists\n\nDownstream serving frameworks and users have flagged gaps in FlashInfer's recent release quality,\nand the pattern is visible directly in our own issue tracker — e.g. attention quality regressions\n(#1645), BF16 MoE stability (#3364), hangs (#3329), and MoE perf (#3521).\n\nThis plan lays out, grounded in that history: **what's broken, why it escapes today's tests, what\nwe're building to catch it before release, and — issue by issue — how much of the historical bug\nstream each piece addresses.** The catch-rate figures are forward-looking estimates — from reviewing\npast issues one at a time and judging what each harness will catch once fully built out, across\nbackends/dtypes/architectures — not a measurement of today's coverage, and are given as ranges.\n\n## Precedent — a proven playbook, not a bet\n\nThe design here is borrowed from cuDNN, where it already worked. After cuDNN shipped\n`test_mhas_v2` — a single consolidated attention fuzzer (one strong harness, an authoritative\nreference, adversarial inputs), the direct model for the design below — attention-related bug\nreports dropped substantially and the time from kernel-completion to end-to-end convergence\nshortened (far fewer silent-correctness round-trips). We are porting that working system to\nFlashInfer, op by op. The sharpest techniques carry over directly:\n\n- **sparse + exact-grid inputs** so structural bugs aren't averaged away (a low-precision analog of\n  the sparse-integer trick);\n- **an authoritative reference recipe** as the single source of truth;\n- **output-buffer poison** for the uninitialized / padding-leak class;\n- **per-backend determinism (CRC) + a device-state probe** after each config;\n- **compute-sanitizer racecheck + SASS forensics** for Heisenbugs (§4).\n\nThe point: this is a portable system, not a one-off. It is now validated on **two** ops (MoE and\nscaled GEMM, §3) — and in both cases it found real bugs shortly after landing.\n\n## 1. Diagnosis — why bugs reach users today\n\nFrom a census of 2026-H1 issues (~131 bug issues, 161 merged fix-PRs):\n\n1. **The dominant clusters are low-precision and MoE.** MoE alone ≈ 31% of bug issues; the\n   low-precision FP4/FP8/MXFP8 GEMM+MoE cluster ≈ 36%; attention/KV is another large slice.\n2. **~60% of fixes land in the Python dispatch / validation / marshaling layer, not deep in the\n   kernel** — i.e. most bugs are integration and contract bugs, not exotic math.\n3. **The test signal was too weak to gate on.** Some classes — notably hangs and timeouts — could\n   pass without surfacing, and the suites that did run were too gentle to fail on real bugs (point\n   4). Together these let bugs reach users even on paths CI nominally exercised. Strengthening the\n   signal so a real bug reliably turns the build red is the highest-leverage fix and is in progress.\n4. **Existing low-precision tests are gentle:** fixed shape grids, loose `cosine > 0.97`, a single\n   autotune mode, no edge inputs / output-poison / determinism / device-state checks.\n\nThe takeaway that shapes everything below: **the same ~7 failure classes recur across every op**\n(§2). So we don't need 7 unrelated test efforts — we need **one invariant \"kit\"** (no-crash,\nreference-numeric, determinism, output-poison, autotune sweep + real-path, device-state probe)\napplied per op, where the only per-op work is the **authoritative reference** and the **input\nmodel**. That kit is now proven on two ops; it generalizes.\n\n## 2. The recurring bug classes (and the oracle that catches each)\n\n| # | Bug class | Representative examples (any op) | Oracle that catches it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| C1 | Numerical wrong-output / accuracy | MoE #3197, #2822; GEMM #3103; fp8 #2431; sampling #2320 | numeric vs authoritative reference + sparse exact-grid inputs |\n| C2 | NaN / Inf where ref is finite | #2595 (cutlass fp8), #1646 (cutlass MoE), sampling #2402 | reference-aware no-NaN |\n| C3 | Crash / IMA / device-assert | MoE #3427, #2776; GEMM #2516, #3085 | no-crash + device-state probe (+ compute-sanitizer, §4) |\n| C4 | Uninitialized / padding-leak into real output | mm_fp4 #2861 | output-buffer poison |\n| C5 | Determinism / intermittent | sampling #3470, #2284; intermittent MoE NaN | per-backend CRC determinism + N-run stress (§4) |\n| C6 | Autotune: tactic / profiling / cache | MoE #3168, #3197; bmm_fp8 #3085 | tactic sweep + autotune-ON real path + cache-coherence |\n| C7 | Arch coverage / dispatch / marshaling | MoE #3466 (missing SM103 cubin), #3359; mm_fp4 #2577 | arch axis in matrix + build-manifest oracle |\n\nThese seven are the engine. Each op below is just *which classes dominate + what the reference is.*\n\n## 3. Per-op map — status, dominant classes, and projected catch\n\nDenominator throughout = **bug issues only** (feature requests / RFEs excluded entirely).\n\n| Op | Harness status | Dominant classes | Representative issues | Projected full-catch |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **MoE** | ✅ merged, **gating by default** (#4475, 2026-08-19); curated seeds + activation-matrix drift check (#4805) *(updated)* | C1, C3, C6, C7 | #3197, #2822, #1646, #3427, #2776, #3168, #3466, #3359 | ~half of in-scope, audited · **4 real bugs found so far** (tally below) |\n| **Scaled GEMM / BMM** | ✅ merged (#3539, 2026-07-14), gating on the H100 lane *(updated)* | C1/C2, C4, C3/C6, C7 | #3103, #2595, #2861, #3085, #2516, #2577 | comparable to MoE; simpler (no routing) · **2 real findings**, ledger holds 1 open entry |\n| **Sampling** | 🟡 seeded fuzzer + χ² gate in review (#5341, @JulianZJN; approved, 455/455 on SM80/90/100) *(updated 2026-10-18)* | C1, C2, C5 | #3470, #2320, #384, #2402, #2284 | high — small, correctness-dominated surface |\n| **Attention / MLA** | to build (largest effort); a POC reject-or-correct paged-prefill fuzzer exists in #4015 (stalled since July) and already found + fixed one bug (#4014) — **contributor wanted** *(updated)* | C1, C4, C3/C7 | #1645, #2208, #2409, #2408, #1971 | moderate — see note |\n| **Norm / RoPE** | to build (cheapest) — **in progress: @0z5a**; rmsnorm output-contract slice #4994 approved, merging on green CI *(updated 2026-10-18)* | C1/overflow, dispatch | #3005, #2271, #730, #194 | moderate, low effort |\n\n*Attention note: the C1/C4 quality-and-correctness bugs (including the #1645 attention quality\nregression) are catchable; the limiter is attention's larger share of perf and\nframework-triggered bugs, which are covered by the complementary tiers in §4 rather than the\nsingle-op harness.*\n\nTwo harnesses are done and gating; the rest are net-new work in the same style.\n\n**MoE harness (DONE — merged, default-ON).** Drives the real unified MoE API with all 7 oracles, a brutal config\nspace (non-pow2 / odd-token / EP-shard / routing-skew, memory-budgeted for parallel CI), a\nknown-failure ledger, and an autotune cache-coherence scenario. It found **2 real bugs in the new\nAPI shortly after landing**: an EP-shard case producing all-zero output (#3547) and a calibrated\nscale silently inflating output (#3548). *(updated)* Since then it went default-ON in CI with the\nsubsumed legacy UTs pruned (#4475), gained curated seeds for the CUTLASS / b12x backends plus a\nCPU check that the documented activation matrix matches the runner declarations (#4805), and\ncaught two more real bugs: a cross-call device-state corruption from an unpredicated finalize\nepilogue (#3957, fixed by #4186) and MxFP8 weight scales missing the 128x4 interleave after the\nrow shuffle (#4087, fix #4093 open).\n\n**Scaled GEMM / BMM harness (DONE — merged #3539).** One fuzzer over the whole scaled-GEMM family\n(bf16 / fp8 / mxfp8 / nvfp4 / mxfp4, `mm` + `bmm`) on the same 7-oracle kit, plus a **cross-backend\nconvention auditor** (each API declares its scale convention; backends sharing a convention must\nagree numerically). It already surfaced real issues: a cuDNN bf16→fp16 miscompute on Hopper for\nnon-power-of-2 M in one cuDNN point release (now guarded inside the FlashInfer API), and a\nboth-backend `bmm_mxfp8` batched scale-factor bug (#3604). **This is the key generalization\ndatapoint** — the MoE playbook ported to a second op and immediately caught bugs, with only the\nreference + input model written fresh.\n\n**Harness-found bugs — running tally** *(new)*. This is the only measured (not projected) catch\ndata in the plan; kept current as fixes land.\n\n| # | Found by | Class | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| #3547 EP-shard all-zero output | MoE | C1 | ✅ fixed |\n| #3548 activation global_scale silently ignored | MoE | C1 | open (fix #3592 closed unmerged) |\n| #3957 cross-call device-state corruption, CuTe finalize N-tile overrun | MoE | C3 | ✅ fixed (#4186) |\n| #4087 MxFP8 weight-scale interleave missing after row shuffle | MoE | C1 | open (fix #4093) |\n| cuDNN bf16→fp16 miscompute, non-pow2 M, Hopper | GEMM | C1 | ✅ guarded in API |\n| #3604 bmm_mxfp8 garbage for b>1, M%128≠0 (cuDNN + CUTLASS) | GEMM | C1/C3 | open (fix #3666); ledgered, crash-capable on cu129 |\n| #4014 cuDNN attention scale missing from graph-cache key | Attention POC (#4015) | C6-like | ✅ fixed |\n| #5339 per-request `top_k`/`min_p` read by output position, not probs row (OOB when `indices` reuses rows) | Sampling (#5341) | C1/C3 | ✅ fixed (#5340) *(new)* |\n| #5347 per-request `top_k` cast to int32 but read as int64 with int64 `indices` (filter disabled + OOB) | Sampling (#5341) | C1/C3 | ✅ fixed (#5348) *(new)* |\n\n**Why catch-rate climbs over time.** The gap between *caught outright* and *at least flagged* is\nmostly shape-luck and intermittence — both are sampling problems. Retiring the now-subsumed per-op\ntests frees CI capacity to fuzz several× longer, and the fuzzers are fully seed-deterministic so any\ncatch reproduces from the test id alone. Curated production shapes (for shape-luck) and N-run stress\n(for intermittence) buy that conversion more cheaply than brute-force time. Neither helps the\ntolerance-floor cases (an oracle-strength problem the Phase 2 re-audit loop would prioritize) nor\nthe no-oracle-exists cases (needs the\nbuild-manifest oracle).\n\n## 4. Force-multipliers — the complementary tiers\n\nA correctness net can't see everything. Alongside one hard precondition (A), we run three\ncomplementary tiers (B–D) that close most of what it structurally can't — these are part of the same\nrelease-quality program, not a separate workstream:\n\n**(A) A robust, gating CI signal + broader architecture coverage — the #1 lever.** Beyond the gentle\ntest content already diagnosed in §1, two infra gaps let bugs through, and both are being closed:\n\n- **Signal robustness / SNR.** CI must reliably catch hangs and timeouts and turn them red, and the\n  low-precision suites must PR-gate (not nightly-only) so a real failure blocks the build rather than\n  passing silently. This directly closes the **hang class** (#769, #3329, #2933-class) — exactly the\n  mode a weak timeout signal hid; a per-test timeout + N-run stress make hangs deterministically red.\n  *(updated)* **Landed:** the unit-test runner now shards with per-unit timeout budgets and a\n  timeout policy, replacing the 7200 s per-file cap (#4141, #4359). Both fuzzers are default-ON.\n  **Caveat that explains most of the theoretical-vs-actual catch gap:** public PR CI has no Blackwell\n  runner (T4 / A10G / H100 only), so the fuzzers gate pre-merge on SM90 only; the Blackwell-only\n  backends — most of the MoE surface — are gated post-merge by the internal daily runs. Until a\n  Blackwell lane exists pre-merge, \"gating\" for those backends means \"red the next day\".\n- **Architecture coverage.** Historically thin, and several escapes were arch-specific (#3466 missing\n  SM103 cubin, #2577 SM120 all-backends-fail, #3359) — this is being actively broadened. Every test\n  axis (and the build-manifest check in Stream 2) should span the full supported SM range.\n\nUntil the signal is both robust and demanding, the catch-rates here stay theoretical — no oracle\nimprovement substitutes for a gate that actually fires.\n\n**(B) Perf-regression tracking** (in progress; no in-tree evidence yet as of 2026-09-06) closes the perf class we exclude from the correctness\nharnesses by design (#3521, #3537, #2992, #2671, #2859, #1753 — perf is ≈10–15% of issues).\n\n**(C) A compute-sanitizer weekly wrap** around the same fuzz configs catches the race / IMA /\ndevice-corruption class that single-run correctness misses, and — key — turns **Heisenbugs into\ndeterministic failures** (#3168, #3427, #3085, intermittent-NaN class, #2776). Near-zero to build —\nthe same tests, just wrapped — and run on a weekly cadence since the sanitizer is slow. *(updated)*\nInternal QA already runs suites under compute-sanitizer (tests started needing sanitizer-aware skips,\n#4493); there is no in-tree workflow yet. (A proven\ntechnique — the same racecheck / SASS forensics approach root-caused a Blackwell matmul buffer-race\nin cuDNN; it transfers directly.)\n\n**(D) Framework-integration tests** (drop a new FlashInfer into known-good vLLM / SGLang and run\ntheir suites — in progress; no in-tree evidence yet; see @ormandj's SM120 TP2 findings below for\nconcrete Tier D / C7 gate candidates, #3930 still open) reproduce the framework-glue / dispatch-triggered class single-op\nharnesses can't: a real kernel bug that only surfaces through a live dispatch + concurrency sequence\n(#3427, #3390, #3329, the framework-observed side of #1645). It also catches higher-level\nintegration / regression bugs (API-contract drift, end-to-end accuracy) no kernel-level oracle sees;\nif the integration matrix includes multi-GPU TP/EP, it reaches into the multi-rank class otherwise\nstructurally out of scope.\n\n**How much this catches.** With the harnesses gating in CI plus the three tiers, the\nprogram would have *touched* (some tier flags it) **a large majority** of historical bug issues. The\nshare that would *reliably gate the build red* — deterministically block a release, not just\nflag-when-lucky — is a smaller core, which the roadmap's curated shapes + N-run stress + longer fuzz\nsteadily grow. The irreducible remainder is structural: **multi-node / multi-rank collectives**\nbeyond the integration matrix (e.g. #3530, #3279) and **build / packaging / air-gapped artifacts**\n(#3466-class). For context, today's effective pre-release catch on the dominant low-precision/MoE\nclass was near-zero — under a signal too weak to gate on.\n\n## 5. Plan of record\n\n**Phase 0 — land what's done.** MoE harness → merged; Scaled GEMM/BMM harness (PR #3539) → merged\n2026-07-14; verify-to-close the harness-found findings as their fixes land (the known-failure ledger\nauto-flags each fix). *(done; 3 findings still open, see tally in §3)*\n\n**Phase 1**\n\n- **Stream 0 — make the CI signal trustworthy + gating.** Strengthen the signal so hangs/timeouts\n  surface; confirm the low-precision suites PR-gate (not nightly-only); triage what newly surfaces.\n  Highest leverage; unblocks everything else. *(updated: per-unit timeout runner landed, fuzzers\n  default-ON with legacy UTs pruned; remaining = a pre-merge Blackwell lane, see Tier A.)*\n- **Stream 1 — replicate the pattern, one tester at a time** (concrete testers, no premature\n  abstraction). Scaled GEMM is **done** (the simplest port — no routing — already finding bugs).\n  Remaining, in the proven style: **(a) Sampling** (cheap, high-yield, distributional reference;\n  **unassigned**) → **(b) Norm/RoPE** (cheap; **@0z5a**, starting with the rmsnorm output-contract\n  slice) → **(c) Attention/MLA** (largest; online-softmax-aware reference + mask/padding poison;\n  directly targets the #1645 quality-regression class; POC in #4015, **unassigned**). Extract a\n  shared invariant-kit only after a third tester reveals the real common shape — the ledger\n  (`tests/test_helpers/fuzz_ledger.py`) is the first piece already shared by both fuzzers.\n- **Stream 2 — complementary tiers.** Perf-regression tracking (in progress) + framework-integration\n  tests (in progress) + a compute-sanitizer weekly wrap + a **build-manifest / arch-coverage check**\n  (every advertised backend×quant×arch actually instantiates a kernel — closes the missing-cubin\n  class).\n\n**Phase 2 — re-audit and iterate.** This is a loop, not a one-shot. Once Phase 1 is in place\n(harnesses + a trustworthy, gating signal + the complementary tiers), re-measure the bug-escape\nstream against the now-stronger gate: what still reaches users? Feed that back into the same\ndiagnose → build → gate cycle, each pass targeting the next-largest remaining escape class — e.g.\ndeepening whichever oracle the re-audit shows is still too weak (a current candidate: the\ncross-backend reference on quantized modes). The re-audit sets the priority, not up-front guesswork.\n\n## Appendix — method & provenance\n\n- **Catch-rate figures** are issue-by-issue estimates given as ranges; non-MoE per-op figures are\n  projections by analogy that become measured as each harness lands.\n- **Issue IDs** in §2–§3 were verified against `flashinfer-ai/flashinfer` (open/closed + titles).\n- **Harnesses in-tree:** unified MoE API fuzzer `tests/moe/test_unified_moe_fuzz.py` (merged,\n  default-ON); unified Scaled GEMM/BMM fuzzer + convention auditor `tests/gemm/test_unified_gemm_fuzz.py`\n  (merged #3539); shared known-failure ledger `tests/test_helpers/fuzz_ledger.py`.\n- **Distinction that matters for reading the numbers:** *touched* (some tier flags the issue) is a\n  larger set than *reliably gates red* (deterministically blocks the release). The roadmap converts\n  the former into the latter over time; the structural remainder (multi-node collectives + packaging)\n  stays out of scope for kernel-level testing.\n\n<!-- note to self: claude::d6f47b32-e07c-43ec-9755-cd807049d528 — \"tracker #3605 status refresh 2026-09-06\"\ncwd /home/scratch.yanxu_libs/flashinfer -->\n\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/3605/reactions","total_count":3,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":2,"rocket":1,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/3605/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5878","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5878/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5878/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5878/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5878","id":5666231934,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABUbvWfg","number":5878,"title":"bug(sampling): top_k_arr/min_p_arr are validated against input rows but indexed by output row, reading out of bounds when indices is longer than probs","user":{"login":"kahyunnam","id":69875166,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5ODc1MTY2","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69875166?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam","html_url":"https://github.com/kahyunnam","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kahyunnam/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":5761844636,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABV27FnA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/good%20first%20issue","name":"good first issue","color":"7057ff","default":true,"description":"Good for newcomers","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":10086440798,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACWTLfXg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/op:%20misc","name":"op: misc","color":"29baf5","default":false,"description":"norm, activation, sampling, RoPE, quantization, etc.","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":3,"created_at":"2026-10-01T17:19:12Z","updated_at":"2026-10-18T09:32:09Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"MEMBER","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Summary\n\n`top_k_sampling_from_probs` and `min_p_sampling_from_probs` validate their per-request parameter tensor against the **input** batch but index it by the **output** row. When `indices` is longer than `probs.size(0)`, every output row at or past `probs.size(0)` reads past the end of the parameter tensor and silently samples with a garbage `top_k` / `min_p`.\n\n- `check_tensor_param(maybe_top_k_arr, probs)` — [`csrc/sampling.cu:216`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/main/csrc/sampling.cu#L216) — requires `top_k_arr.size(0) == probs.size(0)`, via [`csrc/sampling_utils.h:34`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/main/csrc/sampling_utils.h#L34). Same for `min_p` at [`csrc/sampling.cu:262`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/main/csrc/sampling.cu#L262).\n- The kernels read [`top_k_arr[bx]`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/main/include/flashinfer/sampling.cuh#L872) and [`min_p_arr[bx]`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/main/include/flashinfer/sampling.cuh#L1129), where `bx` is `blockIdx.x`, i.e. the output row.\n\nThe sibling kernels get this right and index by the input row: [`top_p_arr[row_idx]`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/main/include/flashinfer/sampling.cuh#L1006) in `TopPSamplingFromProbKernel`, and [`top_k_arr[row_idx]` / `top_p_arr[row_idx]`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/main/include/flashinfer/sampling.cuh#L1228-L1229) in `TopKTopPSamplingFromProbKernel`. So only the two `[bx]` sites are wrong.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nOn a B200 (SM100), 4 input rows, 16 output rows via `indices = arange(16) % 4`, vocab 512. The parameter tensor is a length-4 view carved out of a 4096-element buffer whose tail holds a conspicuous value, so an out-of-bounds read returns that tail instead of faulting:\n\n| Call | Parameter tensor | Output rows violating the parameter |\n|---|---|---|\n| `top_k_sampling_from_probs` | length-4 `top_k`, all `1`, buffer tail `500` | **12 / 16** — exactly rows 4–15 |\n| `min_p_sampling_from_probs` | length-4 `min_p`, all `1.0`, buffer tail `0.0` | **12 / 16** — exactly rows 4–15 |\n| `top_p_sampling_from_probs` (control) | length-4 `top_p`, all `1e-6`, buffer tail `1.0` | **0 / 16** |\n\nWith `top_k = 1` every output row must return `argmax(probs[indices[i]])`; rows 4–15 do not. The `top_p` control isolates the cause to the `bx` / `row_idx` choice rather than anything about the harness.\n\nNote that `compute-sanitizer --tool memcheck` does **not** flag this: the read stays inside the PyTorch caching-allocator segment, so a memcheck-clean run is not evidence of safety here.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Repro script</summary>\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom flashinfer import sampling\n\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\nIN, OUT, V = 4, 16, 512\nprobs = torch.softmax(torch.randn(IN, V, device=\"cuda\"), dim=-1)\nindices = torch.arange(OUT, device=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.int32) % IN\n\n# Length-IN views carved out of a larger buffer whose tail holds a conspicuous value.\nk_buf = torch.full((4096,), 500, dtype=torch.int32, device=\"cuda\")\nk_buf[:IN] = 1\ntop_k = k_buf[:IN]\n\np_buf = torch.zeros(4096, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"cuda\")\np_buf[:IN] = 1.0\nmin_p = p_buf[:IN]\n\ntp_buf = torch.full((4096,), 1.0, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"cuda\")\ntp_buf[:IN] = 1e-6\ntop_p = tp_buf[:IN]\n\nargmax = probs.argmax(dim=-1)\n\n\ndef violations(out):\n    return [(i, int(out[i])) for i in range(OUT) if int(out[i]) != int(argmax[indices[i]])]\n\n\nout_k = sampling.top_k_sampling_from_probs(probs, top_k, indices=indices, deterministic=True)\nprint(\"top_k=1  violations:\", len(violations(out_k)), \"/\", OUT, violations(out_k)[:6])\n\nout_p = sampling.min_p_sampling_from_probs(probs, min_p, indices=indices, deterministic=True)\nprint(\"min_p=1  violations:\", len(violations(out_p)), \"/\", OUT, violations(out_p)[:6])\n\n# Control: top_p indexes by input row, so it must be unaffected.\nout_tp = sampling.top_p_sampling_from_probs(probs, top_p, indices=indices, deterministic=True)\nprint(\"top_p    violations:\", len(violations(out_tp)), \"/\", OUT)\n```\n\n```\ntop_k=1  violations: 12 / 16 [(4, 189), (5, 226), (6, 216), (7, 345), (8, 459), (9, 92)]\nmin_p=1  violations: 12 / 16 [(4, 320), (5, 147), (6, 350), (7, 300), (8, 42), (9, 113)]\ntop_p    violations: 0 / 16\n```\n\n</details>\n\n### Fix\n\nTwo options, and the choice is a contract decision rather than a mechanical one:\n\n1. Index by the input row — change `top_k_arr[bx]` to `top_k_arr[row_idx]` and `min_p_arr[bx]` to `min_p_arr[row_idx]`, matching the existing validation and the two sibling kernels.\n2. Keep output-row indexing and fix the validation to use the output batch instead of `probs`, which is what `top_k`/`min_p` being *per-request* arguably implies.\n\nOption 2 matches what the docstrings suggest (`indices` maps outputs to distributions, so a per-request parameter is naturally per-output) and is the convention #5745 uses for `seed`/`offset`, which validates against `output.size(0)`. Option 1 is the smaller change and keeps all four kernels consistent with each other. Either way the four kernels should agree, and a test with `len(indices) > probs.size(0)` plus a tensor-valued parameter would have caught this.\n\n### Environment\n\nNVIDIA B200 (SM100, CC 10.0), CUDA 13.0, torch 2.14.0+cu130, Python 3.10, `main` @ `1cad38165`. Nothing about this is architecture-specific.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5878/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5878/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254","id":5014567791,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABKuQ7bw","number":4254,"title":"[CAKE] Long-term CAKE-generated Kernel Progress Tracker","user":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":10086440798,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACWTLfXg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/op:%20misc","name":"op: misc","color":"29baf5","default":false,"description":"norm, activation, sampling, RoPE, quantization, etc.","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11804068006,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACv5PIpg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/op:%20linear%20attention","name":"op: linear attention","color":"448150","default":false,"description":"KDA, mamba, GDN, etc. review filtering.","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"yzh119","id":11773619,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExNzczNjE5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11773619?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119","html_url":"https://github.com/yzh119","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},{"login":"hzfan","id":18756967,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NzU2OTY3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18756967?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan","html_url":"https://github.com/hzfan","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},{"login":"xslingcn","id":43036991,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQzMDM2OTkx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/43036991?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn","html_url":"https://github.com/xslingcn","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/xslingcn/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":9,"created_at":"2026-07-29T23:51:09Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T22:51:51Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":{"id":20417977,"node_id":"IT_kwDOCKV4Gs4BN425","name":"Task","description":"A specific piece of work","color":"yellow","created_at":"2024-02-19T14:59:17Z","updated_at":"2024-07-26T15:25:38Z","is_enabled":true},"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":45,"completed":23,"percent_completed":51},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"To track multi-platform kernels generated from CAKE 🍰 harness + claude/codex agent. \n\nWe are more than happy to take any new kernel challenges!\n\n**Feel free to reach out/leave your comments/create sub-issues here to submit your customized CAKE kernel request. And we will bake it for you!**\n\n**We know many of you have been asking for training kernels—and we heard you.** You have had your CAKE going forward; now it is time to take it **back**! Follow the dedicated forward & backward tracker at #4642, bring us your toughest training bottleneck, and let us bake the full pass together. 🍰🔥\n\nAnd we recommend using agent to index the kernels you need. 😜\n\n## Kernel categories\n\nPull requests are tracked in the category sub-issues below, with detailed sections and links to each PR. Please add new PRs to the corresponding category.\n\n### Communication\n\nSub-issue: #6593\n\nMoE all-reduce/finalize and residual-add/RMSNorm/combine fusion; MNNVL, Ulysses and DCP all-to-all; all-gather matmul and sequence parallelism; QKV/Norm/RoPE and MiniMax ICP exchange; FP8 GEMM + all-reduce; NVFP4-transport TP all-reduce with residual + RMSNorm fusion; Kimi-K3 TP12 tails, SM120 PCIe communication and MoK.\n\n### Attention\n\nSub-issue: #6594\n\nFMHA variants (paged/DCP/GQA, MiniMax sparse, NVFP4, Sage/VSA, and MiniMax-H3 dense/packed-varlen); MLA K concatenation, decode and sparse MLA, including DSA forward/backward. Also covers fused pre-attention/output projection, QKV quantize-and-pack, QK RMSNorm/RoPE/KV append, Kimi-K3 AttnRes, and DSA indexer logits/top-k.\n\n### Linear Attention & State Space\n\nSub-issue: #6595\n\nRecurrent KDA prefill, decode and training; Gated Delta Rule context-parallel and non-CP prefill, FP32-state paths and the source-only Blackwell backend; Mamba SSDCombined and selective state update.\n\n### GEMM / BMM\n\nSub-issue: #6596\n\nBF16 BMM, BF16 x FP4/NVFP4/FP8 and grouped GEMM, DeepGEMM/TinyGEMM2, all-gather matmul and router GEMM. Includes SVDQuant, runtime BF16 LoRA, per-token quantize + GEMM, projection gradients, MiniMax-H3 FC1 + SwiGLU/full-MLP fusion, and Kimi-K3 FP8 projection.\n\n### MoE & Routing\n\nSub-issue: #6597\n\nFused MoE, AlphaMoE, MegaMoE and BGMV MoE; DeepSeek fused routing; Kimi-K3 Stable LatentMoE and fused router; warp-decode kernels.\n\n### Activation & Quantization\n\nSub-issue: #6598\n\nMXFP8 quantization, fused SwiGLU + MXFP8 quantization, softmax, and RMSNorm training kernels for forward and backward passes.\n\n### LM-head & Loss\n\nSub-issue: #6599\n\nChunked LM-head with fused cross-entropy or policy loss, including memory-bounded backward for training.\n\n### Sampling\n\nSub-issue: #6600\n\nFused radix top-k/top-p sampling, architecture qualification, kernel and dispatch optimizations, source/loader refactoring, and CUDA graph replay tests.\n\n### Vision Encoder\n\nSub-issue: #6601\n\nKimi-K3 vision tower kernels for MoonViT-3D and PatchMergerV2.\n\n### Cross-family Support\n\nSub-issue: #6602\n\nRubin/SM107 support and tuning across GDN, KDA, routing, BGMV MoE and MLA K concatenation; MiniMax-H3 SM120 shared kernels/helpers.\n\n### Standalone Build of CAKE Packages\n\nSub-issue: #6553\n\nThe seven CAKE packages (`cake_rmsnorm_train`, `cake_dsa_train`, `cake_dsa_indexer`, `cake_lm_head_loss`, `dense_projection_gemm`, `cake_moe_grouped_gemm`, `cake_mok_bf16`) build and test outside flashinfer without patches: `build.json` manifests and a shared loader, header-free bindings, behaviour switches as keyword arguments, and backend test suites that import only the package.\n\n- #6571\n- #6584\n- #6610\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254/reactions","total_count":26,"+1":18,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":8,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5768","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5768/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5768/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5768/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5768","id":5654559818,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABUQm8Sg","number":5768,"title":"[Tracking] Deslop Cake-generated kernels (smaller, reviewable generated code)","user":{"login":"yzh119","id":11773619,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExNzczNjE5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/11773619?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119","html_url":"https://github.com/yzh119","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yzh119/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":1,"created_at":"2026-09-30T23:50:13Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T13:05:25Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":75,"completed":74,"percent_completed":98},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Background\n\nMany kernels generated by Cake have been upstreamed into FlashInfer over the past months. They live in `csrc/cake_*`, `flashinfer/experimental/*`, and parts of `csrc/kda`, `csrc/gdn`, and `csrc/fused_moe`. The kernels are fast, but the way they are delivered does not meet the software-engineering bar of the rest of the library:\n\n- The 244 PRs from the automated export account added about 8.0M lines and removed about 1.5M. The files those PRs added that are still on `main` come to 4,441 files and 4.9M lines. Of those, 3,522 are C++/CUDA/PTX files with 4.1M lines.\n- A single PR routinely adds 10^4–10^5 lines. Examples: #5523 (+466K), #5697 (+178K), #5183 (+162K), #5684 (+157K / −77K), and #5694 (+142K / −17K).\n- These PRs cannot really be reviewed. They also bloat the source distribution and the JIT cache, and they make regressions hard to bisect.\n\n## What is wrong\n\nExamples below are from `main` at 45d35e4e7.\n\n1. **Unused kernel instances.** Some sources are never registered, or cannot be reached from any dispatch path. Superseded generations were never deleted.\n2. **Near-duplicate kernels in separate files.** For example, `csrc/cake_trtllm_moe_allreduce_union` has 322 kernel files. After normalising the generated hash names and the architecture tag, only 48 distinct bodies remain.\n3. **Per-architecture copies.** Several families keep SM100a and SM103a trees that are identical or differ by a line or two. For example, in `flashinfer/experimental/kimi_k3_latent_moe` each SM100a/SM103a kernel pair differs only in the symbol name and one shared-memory address-conversion line.\n4. **Problem sizes and launch configs baked in as constants.** For example, `csrc/cake_minimax_h3_mxfp8_pre_attention` has 176 kernel files. That is 2 schedules × 44 values of `#define M` × 2 architectures.\n5. **Hard-coded strides and contiguity.** These force `.contiguous()` copies on the Python side, or make the op reject views.\n6. **Host overhead.**\n   - The Python hot path does per-call device-property queries, workspace allocations, tensor construction, and dict/string work.\n   - Generated C++ bindings carry their own device-guard classes, mutexes, and caches instead of using `tvm_ffi_utils.h`.\n7. **Other bloat.** Multi-thousand-line generated Python registries and dispatch tables, per-architecture generated dispatchers, large metadata JSON files, raw PTX blobs, and README benchmark dumps.\n\n## Plan\n\nThere are two tracks.\n\n**Fix the generator and exporter so new exports are lean by construction.**\n\n- One source per schedule, shared across architectures. Architecture differences go behind `__CUDA_ARCH__` guards only where they are real.\n- Runtime problem sizes and strides instead of per-shape clones.\n- Only export the instances that dispatch can reach.\n- Emit identical bodies once.\n- Keep bindings thin and built on `tvm_ffi_utils.h`.\n- Cache host-side planning, with no syncs or allocations on the hot path.\n- Add export-time checks that reject all of the patterns above.\n\n**Clean up what is already on `main`, family by family.** Each task gets a sub-issue below with measured findings and a concrete plan. Cleanup PRs follow these rules:\n\n- **Mechanical steps**: deleting unreachable sources, merging per-architecture copies, and deduplicating identical bodies.\n  - They change neither behaviour nor performance.\n  - Equivalence is shown by comparing the compiled SASS before and after, in addition to the existing tests.\n- **Structural steps**: runtime shapes and strides, and consolidating clones into one template.\n  - These are regenerated by the exporter, not edited by hand.\n  - They come with per-shape before/after benchmarks.\n- Every PR is small enough to review, and has a negative net line count.\n\nSub-issues are added below as each family's analysis completes.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5768/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5768/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682","id":5898590425,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABX5VY2Q","number":6682,"title":"[cake deslop] Round 2 tracking: 49 family audits of Cake-generated code on main (2026-10-17)","user":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-17T13:01:32Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T13:01:40Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":49,"completed":9,"percent_completed":18},"parent_issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5768","issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"Second audit pass over every Cake-generated family on `main` @ 3184bbea6e41290320237fbda2ed87b05661881e (2026-10-17), after the 74 first-round sub-issues of #5768 (#5769–#5844) were closed by their PRs. Each family issue below lists what is still dispatch-unreachable, duplicated, per-arch-copied, shape-baked or host-heavy, with the dispatch code that proves it, the tests that pin it and the mechanical-first steps to remove it.\n\nThis tracker exists because #5768 reached GitHub's limit of 100 sub-issues per parent; the round-2 family issues are attached here, and this issue is attached to #5768. Work proceeds in the order below (largest expected reduction first); each mechanical step ships as its own PR gated on per-symbol SASS equivalence of the surviving kernels and the existing tests on the target GPUs.\n\n| # | Issue | Family | Expected reduction (lines) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | #6633 | cake_sampling | 368,204 |\n| 2 | #6634 | cake_mok_bf16 | 289,791 |\n| 3 | #6635 | cake_mxfp4_situ_moe | 272,000 |\n| 4 | #6636 | cake_fmha round 2 | 260,009 |\n| 5 | #6637 | cake_dsv4 SM120 | 202,323 |\n| 6 | #6638 | cake_gdn (non-CP) round 2 | 182,500 |\n| 7 | #6639 | csrc/kda root + PTX (2) | 135,047 |\n| 8 | #6640 | cake_vsa round 2 | 130,150 |\n| 9 | #6641 | cake_warp_decode (2) | 109,300 |\n| 10 | #6642 | blackwell_bf16_fp4 | 96,600 |\n| 11 | #6643 | cake_mamba_ssd_combined + cake_selective_state_update (2) | 90,977 |\n| 12 | #6644 | cake_dsv4 SM100/SM103 (2) | 70,121 |\n| 13 | #6645 | deepgemm_mqa | 63,000 |\n| 14 | #6646 | cake_minimax_h3 round 2 | 44,839 |\n| 15 | #6647 | cake_bgmv_moe | 39,470 |\n| 16 | #6648 | cake_msa_icp_indexer | 26,548 |\n| 17 | #6649 | cake_gdn CP prefill (2) | 24,859 |\n| 18 | #6650 | cake_fused_qk_rope_append (bf16/fp8) + concat_mla_kv_quant_fp8 | 24,826 |\n| 19 | #6651 | KDA prepared BF16 prefill (2) | 189,800 |\n| 20 | #6652 | cake_kimi_k3_attn_res | 108,487 |\n| 21 | #6653 | kimi_k3_fp8_projection (2) | 100,700 |\n| 22 | #6654 | blackwell_msa/msa_nvfp4_decode (2) | 88,400 |\n| 23 | #6655 | cake_nvfp4_per_token | 72,000 |\n| 24 | #6656 | cake_dsa_indexer | 67,900 |\n| 25 | #6657 | cake_sage_block_sparse_attention (2) | 60,981 |\n| 26 | #6658 | cake_stepfun | 58,200 |\n| 27 | #6659 | kimi_k3_vision_tower | 48,700 |\n| 28 | #6660 | grouped FP8 GEMM families | 42,900 |\n| 29 | #6661 | KDA TF32 prefill (csrc/kda/tf32), round 2 | 40,829 |\n| 30 | #6662 | cake_kimi_k3_situ | 31,000 |\n| 31 | #6663 | deepgemm fp8/fp4/kgroup/mixed/batched GEMMs (2) | 30,500 |\n| 32 | #6664 | MegaMoE (round 2) | 28,600 |\n| 33 | #6665 | dense_projection_gemm | 28,000 |\n| 34 | #6666 | small GEMMs | 26,731 |\n| 35 | #6667 | cake_minimax_h3 SM100/103 (2) | 25,482 |\n| 36 | #6668 | Kimi-K3 MoE round 2 | 24,700 |\n| 37 | #6669 | mega_gate/mhc/moe_v3 (2) | 22,552 |\n| 38 | #6670 | cake_trtllm_mla_blackwell + cake_kimi_k3_mla (2) | 19,973 |\n| 39 | #6671 | cake_lm_head_loss | 16,880 |\n| 40 | #6672 | policy cross-cut | 15,877 |\n| 41 | #6673 | nvfp4_mla_decode + cake_mla_varq_dcp_decode (2) | 15,479 |\n| 42 | #6674 | cake_comm (2) | 13,614 |\n| 43 | #6675 | cake_all_gather_matmul + cake_qkv_norm_rope_exchange_overlap | 12,993 |\n| 44 | #6676 | balanced_gqa_decode (2) | 10,742 |\n| 45 | #6677 | cake_dsa_train (2) | 8,740 |\n| 46 | #6678 | KDA Python variants | 7,696 |\n| 47 | #6679 | sm110_xqa + sm110_gqa_decode (round 2) | 52,000 |\n| 48 | #6680 | cake_blackwell_softmax + cake_rmsnorm_train | 6,780 |\n| 49 | #6681 | cake_moe_grouped_gemm | 3,833 |\n\nTotal expected reduction after all steps: about 3,711,633 lines (mechanical steps first; structural regeneration steps are gated on per-shape before/after benchmarks).\n\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6680","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6680/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6680/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6680/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6680","id":5898579449,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABX5Ut-Q","number":6680,"title":"[cake deslop] cake_blackwell_softmax + cake_rmsnorm_train: delete 3 unreachable softmax kernels, fold clones, slim registries (-6.8K lines)","user":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-17T13:00:06Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T13:00:16Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"parent_issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682","issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"**Background.** This issue covers the small Cake families that no other #5768 sub-issue covers. `cake_blackwell_softmax` was added in #4282 (2026-09-23) and has never been deslopped. It backs `flashinfer.sampling.softmax` on CC 10.0/10.3 through one JIT module, `blackwell_softmax` (`flashinfer/jit/cake_blackwell_softmax.py`, also registered for AOT at `flashinfer/aot.py:1066`). That module is built from 6 `csrc/cake_blackwell_softmax*.cu` files (4,165 lines, 14 `__global__` kernels) plus `include/flashinfer/cake_blackwell_softmax.cuh` (108 lines). `cake_rmsnorm_train` was added in #5741 (2026-10-05), after the first round. It serves `cake_rmsnorm_train_forward`/`_backward`/`cake_rmsnorm` on CC 10.0/10.3/10.7. #6571 moved it into `flashinfer/cake_rmsnorm_train_backend` behind a thin facade, #6584 re-rendered it with `build.json` and the shared `cake_build.py` loader (both under #6553), and #6610 added keyword-arg switches. The package has 52 files and 13,061 lines: 21 kernels (6,100 lines), 21 bindings (2,241), `cake_jit.py` (2,398, of which 1,875 are the `MODULES` literal) and `build.json` (1,124 lines, 46 libraries: 21 kernels delivered on sm_100a 15 / sm_103a 16 / sm_107a 15). `cake_deepseek_fused_routing` (`fused_topk_deepseek(backend=\"cake\")`) was folded by #5788, closed by #5906 (+276/−7,963), into 2 templates plus 36 explicit instantiations (518 + 60 lines). Its csrc has not changed since: #6238 added CC 10.7 to the Python contract and #6171 added cross-backend tests. Line numbers below refer to main @ 3184bbea.\n\n**Findings**\n- **3 softmax kernels are compiled but never selected.** `csrc/cake_blackwell_softmax_cached_cluster.cu` (2,377 lines) defines 10 `cached_c{1,2,4,8,16}_t{4,8}` kernels. `select_cached_variant` (`csrc/cake_blackwell_softmax.cu:118-158`) never returns `(1,8)`, `(2,8)` or `(4,8)`:\n  - the tiles=4 small-chunk candidate exists and wins for every vocab ≤ 65,536;\n  - above that, the wide-chunk candidates start at `(8,8)`, and spreading only grows C;\n  - the `any` fallback is dead because `use_cached_cluster_kernel` caps vocab at the `(16,8)` capacity (262,144).\n\n  Enumerating every vocab multiple of 8 up to that cap, across a range of row and SM counts, returns exactly the other 7 variants. Nothing else references the 3 symbols, and `tests/utils/test_sampling.py` asserts only route id 5. The removal is 683 lines, including header declarations and dispatch cases.\n- **The cluster kernels reduce to 2 bodies.** After normalising names:\n  - `cX_t4` and `cX_t8` differ in 6 lines (tile loop bounds);\n  - `c2`/`c4`/`c8`/`c16` differ in 10 lines (`gridDim.x / C`, `bid / C`, lane compares against `C-1`);\n  - `c1` and `c2` differ in 68 lines (no cluster vs DSM exchange).\n\n  The source headers state that the sm_100a and sm_103a payloads were byte-identical. The only arch-guarded code is 28 `#if __CUDA_ARCH__ == 1030` blocks (20 of them in the cluster file), each wrapping one `griddepcontrol` instruction.\n- **Route 4 is an exact-shape route.** `csrc/cake_blackwell_softmax.cu:88-95` selects the 235-line `csrc/cake_blackwell_softmax_*_exp2.cu` kernel (hard-coded vocab 32000, 512 threads, 16 vectors/thread) only when all of these hold: SM103, vocab == 32000, no temperature, PDL off, and rows ∈ {128, 512, 1024}.\n  - The default call never takes this route. `flashinfer.sampling.softmax` resolves `enable_pdl=None` to `device_support_pdl()` (`sampling.py:957-958`), which is true for major ≥ 9 (`utils.py:869-873`). Only an explicit `enable_pdl=False` reaches it. Every other shape already uses CachedCluster (route 5).\n  - A dispatcher comment (73-76) says the kernel is \"still measured ahead\" on those rows. This has not been re-measured.\n  - Pinned by `flashinfer/sampling.py:124` and by `tests/utils/test_sampling.py` lines 145-158 and 180-202 and the `(512, 32000, None, False, 4)` param at 204-236. These see route 4 because the test hook defaults `enable_pdl=False`.\n- **21 rmsnorm_train bindings, 11 distinct launch configs.** The 21 bindings (17 × 101 + 4 × 131 = 2,241 lines) differ only in `dim3 block(...)` and `config.dynamicSmemBytes`. Both values are already in `MODULES[...][\"launch\"]` (11 distinct values), and the grid comes from Python.\n  - The bindings use the package-private `cake_ffi_checks.h` plus `tvm::ffi::CUDADeviceGuard`/`TVMFFIEnvGetStream`, not `csrc/tvm_ffi_utils.h`.\n  - Every binding includes `cake_rmsnorm_train_host_common.cuh` and adds a `using` line. The header's body is an empty `namespace cake_host_shim_common { }` and it only re-includes `cake_ffi_checks.h`, which every binding already includes (29 lines + 42 lines).\n  - Sources are digest-bound (`cake_build.py:_verified_sources` 168-180), so any edit needs a `build.json` re-render.\n- **6 rmsnorm_train kernels are constant clones (1,049 lines).** Each of the following files under `flashinfer/cake_rmsnorm_train_backend/csrc/` is 4-8 normalised lines from a sibling:\n  - `308da182` vs `03bdf566`: `__launch_bounds__` min-blocks 4;\n  - `2a42e5c0` and `3430e5d9` vs `7673978d`: THREADS 256/128/64, ROWS_PER_CTA 8/4/2, loop form;\n  - `60f47c5d` vs `3d20ab4a`: THREADS 256/512, ROWS_PER_CTA 8/16;\n  - `cf1d0554` vs `bad4db91`: `ld.global.L2::cache_hint` vs plain load;\n  - `c5a8099e` (sm_103a only) vs `b44107e0`: an `#if __CUDA_ARCH__ == 1000` loop branch plus the cache hint.\n- **Hard-coded hidden sizes.** `HIDDEN_SIZES = (512, 2048, 6144)` (`cake_jit.py:2035`). Residual routes exist only for 6144, so `cake_rmsnorm(x, w, residual=u)` with H = 512 or 2048 raises `ValueError` (`__init__.py:364-374`). The kernels hard-code the width (`obase = row64 * 512`).\n- **Generated registries.**\n  - `MODULES` (`cake_jit.py:52-1926`) has 31 records over 21 libraries. 10 of them are `__wide`/`__quad`/... aliases that change only `select_rule`/`grid_rule`, and `arg_plan` and `logical` have only 2 distinct values each. No record is dead, and the row bands have no gaps or overlaps.\n  - In `build.json`, each of the 46 entries (23 lines) repeats 10 identical lines, even though top-level `common` and `arch_flags` blocks already exist. The per-entry `gencode` field is never read (`cake_build.py:jit_spec` 199-205) and is required only by `ENTRY_KEYS` (`scripts/check_cake_build_manifests.py:99`). The sha256 closures cover the 2 `.cu` files per library but not the 4 shared headers.\n  - `flashinfer/__init__.py:259-268` imports the package eagerly.\n- **36 redundant routing instantiations.** `cake_deepseek_fused_routing_kernels.cu:440-516` (77 lines) explicitly instantiates 36 kernels: 9 dtype pairs × `grouped<1,1>`, `grouped<4,8>`, `single<128>`, `single<384>`. These are already instantiated implicitly:\n  - the file is only `#include`d into `csrc/fused_moe/noAuxTcKernels.cu:13` under `FLASHINFER_CAKE_BACKEND`;\n  - `launch<ScoreT,BiasT>` (`cake_deepseek_fused_routing_launch.cuh:40-57`) takes the address of all four templates;\n  - the dispatcher calls `invokeNoAuxTc` for all 9 pairs with no guards (416-494).\n\n  Separately, the 5 bf16 `INSTANTIATE_NOAUX_TC` lines (327-334) sit under `#ifdef ENABLE_BF16`, which neither dsv3 module defines.\n- **Per-call host work.**\n  - rmsnorm_train: `get_device_capability` runs at `__init__.py:55/365/524` and at `cake_jit.py:2299` on every launch. `CakeRMSNormFunction.backward` (608-622) passes no workspace, so every backward allocates with `torch.zeros`.\n  - softmax dispatcher: queries the device and SM count on every CachedCluster/Bootstrap call (360-367, 393-397), queries occupancy on Bootstrap (384-387), and calls `cudaFuncSetAttribute` on every 16-CTA launch (261-267). `#include <flashinfer/sampling.cuh>` compiles `OnlineSoftmax` a second time.\n- **Strides.** rmsnorm_train forces contiguous inputs and outputs, with a temporary buffer and `copy_` (`_kernel_input` 108-132 requires 32 B alignment forward and 16 B backward; `_copy_back` 192-195). Softmax needs contiguous fp32 logits with a 32 B-aligned base, otherwise it falls back (`csrc/cake_blackwell_softmax.cu:98-102`). Routing rejects strided views (pinned by `test_dsv3_fused_routing.py:581-604`).\n- **Tests and checks that pin the layout.**\n  - `tests/norm/test_cake_rmsnorm_train.py::test_registry_records_reference_the_build_manifest` (694-729) asserts `library == kernel`, `sources == [<lib>_kernel.cu, <lib>_binding.cu]`, per-entry `--prec-div=true` and `entry == \"run\"` (728-729), and that `cake_ffi_checks.h` exists.\n  - `scripts/check_cake_build_manifests.py` requires `cake_build.py` to be byte-identical across the 7 `cake_build` packages and one entry per (library, arch).\n  - `test_forward_programs_switch_on_row_count` (324-355) guards any record removal.\n- **Exporter artifacts shared with other packages.** `cake_build.py` is byte-identical in 7 packages (7 × 324 lines), and `cake_ffi_checks.h` exists in 4 variants across them. The exporter also emits the same empty `host_common.cuh` in `csrc/cake_grouped_mxfp8_quantize`, `csrc/cake_concat_mla_kv_quant_fp8` and `flashinfer/experimental/kimi_k3_tp12_tail`.\n\n**Proposed steps**\n- [ ] Delete the 3 unreachable cluster kernels (3 files, −683):\n  - `cake_blackwell_softmax_cached_cluster.cu` lines 293-469, 708-945 and 1184-1421, plus the provenance comments at 31-34/39-42/47-50;\n  - the `.cuh` declarations at 66-70, 76-80 and 86-90;\n  - the three `FLASHINFER_CACHED_SOFTMAX_CASE(*, 8)` lines in `csrc/cake_blackwell_softmax.cu` (165/167/169).\n\n  No test pins them. Confirm that `select_cached_variant` still returns only the 7 kept variants, that the per-symbol SASS of the remaining kernels is unchanged on sm_100a/sm_103a, and that `tests/utils/test_sampling.py -k blackwell` passes on B200 and B300.\n- [ ] Delete `cake_deepseek_fused_routing_kernels.cu:440-516` and keep `#undef CAKE_INF` at 518 (−77). Optionally also drop the dead `ENABLE_BF16` block in `noAuxTcKernels.cu` (8 lines, not counted). SASS identity has so far been argued only from source. Confirm with `cuobjdump -sass` that `dsv3_fused_routing_cake` still has the same 36 symbols with identical SASS, and run `test_dsv3_fused_routing.py -k 'backend_correctness or cuda_graph or matches_default'` on B200.\n- [ ] In one `build.json` re-render, delete the empty `cake_rmsnorm_train_host_common.cuh` and its 42 include/`using` lines (−71). Drop the dead per-entry `gencode` field and relax `ENTRY_KEYS` in `scripts/check_cake_build_manifests.py:99` to match (−46). Run the `tests/norm` Cake suites and the manifest checker.\n- [ ] Fold the 7 remaining cluster kernels into `template<int C, int T>` with two bodies (C == 1 without a cluster, C > 1 with DSM), one PDL block per body, and the `extern \"C\"` symbols kept for the dispatcher table. This takes 1,694 lines down to ~450 lines plus 7 instantiations (−1,100). Diff the SASS of each instantiation on sm_100a/sm_103a. Benchmark vocab {8K, 32K, 64K, 128K, 256K} × rows {1, 16, 128, 1024}, with and without temperature.\n- [ ] Retire route 4 only if a B300 benchmark shows CachedCluster within noise or faster. Benchmark rows {128, 512, 1024} × V = 32000, fp32, no temperature, with an explicit `enable_pdl=False`. In one PR (about −300):\n  - remove `csrc/cake_blackwell_softmax_*_exp2.cu`;\n  - remove the dispatcher lines 32, 38, 64, 73-95, 223-225 and 340-347;\n  - turn the fail-closed special case at 529-531 into an unconditional fallback (behaviour change: `cudaErrorNotSupported` then always falls back to `OnlineSoftmax`);\n  - remove header lines 29-31 and 49-52 and `flashinfer/sampling.py:124`;\n  - update the pins in `tests/utils/test_sampling.py`: 145-158, 180-202, the param at 204-236 with its skip at 224-225, and the comment at 262.\n- [ ] Replace the 21 rmsnorm_train bindings with one forward launcher and one backward launcher. They take block size and dynamic smem from a per-kernel constexpr table, or as scalars from `MODULES[...][\"launch\"]`. Re-render the digests and sources (−1,900). The kernel `.cu` files and their SASS must stay byte-identical. In the same PR, update the `sources == [kernel.cu, binding.cu]` assertion. Run the `tests/norm` Cake suites on B200, B300 and an sm_107a machine, plus the manifest checker.\n- [ ] Compact the registry (about −1,650 beyond the `gencode` drop):\n  - hoist the 2 `arg_plan`/`logical` variants out of the 31 `MODULES` records;\n  - emit the 10 alias records as `{base, select_rule, grid_rule}` deltas;\n  - hoist the identical `nvcc_flags`/`nvcc_flags_if`/`entry` (and the per-arch `min_cuda`) into `common`/`arch_flags`;\n  - relax `ENTRY_KEYS` and `test_cake_rmsnorm_train.py:728-729`.\n\n  Confirm that `selected_modules()`, `launch_grid()` and `workspace_layout()` give identical results for every (arch, route, row switch point).\n- [ ] Fold the 6 clone kernels into their siblings and regenerate (−950). This needs exporter template parameters for THREADS/ROWS_PER_CTA, `__launch_bounds__` min-blocks, cache hint on/off, persistent vs single-row loop, and the sm_100a-only loop branch. Inline-PTX cache hints and loop forms may not round-trip through template parameters. Confirm with a SASS diff per (kernel, arch) on sm_100a/sm_103a/sm_107a, and run `benchmarks/bench_cake_rmsnorm_train.py` at the row switch points for hidden 512/2048/6144.\n- [ ] Follow-ups (not counted here):\n  - share one `cake_build.py` and one `cake_ffi_checks.h` across the 7 packages, which requires changing `check_loader_identity`, or document why each package stays independent;\n  - stop emitting empty `host_common.cuh` (4 packages);\n  - decide whether `closure_sha256` should cover the shared headers;\n  - import `cake_rmsnorm_train` lazily in `flashinfer/__init__.py`;\n  - cache the per-call device queries.\n\n**Expected size:** about 17.9K lines in scope (13,061 rmsnorm_train package + 4,273 softmax + 578 routing) → about 17.0K after the mechanical steps (−877) → about 11.1K after the template, registry and route-4 steps (−6,780 total). The template and registry steps account for about 5,650 of those lines and assume the Cake exporter can emit these parameters. If it cannot, they become hand-maintained templates with re-rendered digests.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6680/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6680/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6660","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6660/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6660/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6660/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6660","id":5898497407,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABX5Ptfw","number":6660,"title":"[cake deslop] grouped FP8 GEMM families: drop 12 exact-point programs, table-drive the 5.4K-line route chain, share helpers, fold clones","user":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-17T12:50:09Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T12:50:20Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"parent_issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682","issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"**Background.** Four Cake-generated families serve the FP8 grouped/batched GEMM paths on SM100/SM103. Each program is one kernel source plus one binding source, shared by `sm_100a` and `sm_103a`. The families are:\n- `cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8`, behind `batch_deepgemm_fp8_nt_groupwise(backend=\"cake\")`.\n- `cake_grouped_fp8_gemm`, behind `prepare_group_gemm_fp8_nt_groupwise_contiguous`.\n- `cake_grouped_fp8_fused_silu_quant`, behind `prepare_group_gemm_fp8_nt_groupwise_contiguous_silu_quant`.\n- `cake_grouped_mxfp8_quantize`, behind `mxfp8_grouped_quantize(backend=\"cake\")`. This backend is explicit only; the default is `cutile`.\n\n`flashinfer/aot.py` also builds grouped and silu for `sm_100a`; the other two families are JIT only. Together they are 146 files / 78,305 lines: csrc 64,652 (batch 38,332, grouped 17,930, silu 7,094, mxfp8 1,296), Python 10,695, dedicated tests 2,027, benchmarks 931. All 60 kernel and 60 binding sources are compiled, and none is orphaned.\n\nThe first round covered three of the families. #5993 (+6,083/−18,356) closed #5817 (silu) and #5818 (grouped). It regenerated both with one source per program and tvm-ffi bindings, but it kept the exact `(m,n,k)` routes and the helper text inlined in every file. #5951 closed #5819 (mxfp8). It caches target resolution but leaves two dtype copies of the kernel. Since then, #6044 (+45,896) added the batch family (38 programs, 76 files, a 6,381-line rendered dispatch table), which never had a sub-issue. #6048 (+5,811/−308) added 4 block-scaled UE8M0 programs and per-call rebinding to `cake_grouped_fp8_gemm`.\n\n**Findings**\n- **Exact-point batch programs.** 12 of the 38 `cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8` programs (11,309 csrc lines) are reachable only through routes that pin `(B,M,N,K,expected_m)` to a single benchmark point, for example `B==1 and M==128 and N==4096 and K==7168 and expected_m == 102`. Overall, 26 of the 84 returning routes are exact benchmark points. No AOT, jit-cache, test, benchmark or doc references these hashes.\n  - **masked_bn.** The 5 masked_bn programs (`0924c6`, `1b7b52`, `1eaf7d`, `2ac9e2`, `e306f2`; 5,454 lines) appear only in 8 exact routes, and a banded route also covers each of those points. Another 12 exact blocks (12 × 38 = 456 lines) select programs that the bands select anyway.\n  - **Swap-AB.** The 6 swap-AB programs (`87a3c5`, `f376ef`, `1bb4c4`, `b53863`, `fffc74`, `215f20`; 4,977 lines) are used only by the 7 `seed_swap_*` routes, which are the only 2-stage workspace routes. `215f20` hard-codes N=6144, K=7168 and 32 groups for the single point `(32,4096,6144,7168)`. Band exclusions keep two sets of shapes off the bands, so those shapes have no route other than swap:\n    - sm_100a: `(B,M)` ∈ {(1,8192), (1,16384), (4,1024)} at `(4096,7168)`.\n    - sm_103a: `(1,16384,4096,7168)`.\n  - **`eb727f`.** This program (878 lines) is reachable only through the `expected_m == 24` clause of `seed_large_nk_cta1`. Its `M<=256` clause never fires because the seven `n7168_k2048` bands come first, and their bn240 band ends in an open `>= 61` CTA tail.\n- **Rendered route chain.** 5,351 of the 6,381 lines in `flashinfer/jit/gemm/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8.py` are rendered first-match predicates: `_routes_sm_100a_148` (L1008-3879, 47 routes) and `_routes_sm_103a_152` (L3880-6358, 37 routes).\n  - The 29 banded routes have the same names and structure in both chains. They differ only in the SM literal (148/152) and the `n7168_k2048` BLOCK_N thresholds.\n  - Some route text is never selected: two `NotImplementedError` stubs (L3548-3559, L6054-6059), the `seed_n7168_k2048` blocks (72 lines), and about 25 lines of shadowed `seed_swap_m224_packed` disjuncts.\n- **SM-count gate.** `DISPATCH_TABLES` (L6359-6362) holds only `(sm_100a, 148)` and `(sm_103a, 152)`. `_resolve` (`flashinfer/gemm/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8.py` L224-228) rejects any other SM count, even though every grid is already `min(..., SM)`.\n  - #6044 states this is by design for 148-SM B200 and 152-SM GB300.\n  - However, `cake_sampling.py` L77 records a 148-SM sm_103a \"B300\", which this backend would reject. Confirm with `multi_processor_count` on GB300/B300 nodes.\n  - The sm_103a/148 branch of `serving_config` (L90-91) is dead.\n- **Batch constant clones.** After normalising hashes and whitespace, 29 of 38 kernels and 36 of 38 bindings are clones of one another:\n  - BLOCK_N 9-cluster `2c120b/741ad4/929a55/a51ac2/a6a50e/c1541d/d22ea5/e6e78d/fe5a14` (9,351 lines).\n  - 4-cluster `02cd3d/20c8e0/54ec41/59edc7` (4,284 lines).\n  - The 8 serving programs (8,842 lines).\n  - Swap GEMM `1bb4c4/b53863/fffc74` (3,345 lines).\n\n  The constants are compiled into loop bounds and address arithmetic (for example, a panel loop of `< 7` vs `< 3`). Folding therefore needs regeneration; substituting `#define`s is not enough.\n- **Hard-coded serving shapes.** The 8 serving kernels take `num_groups`, N and K as parameters but ignore them at 12 `((1) ? const : expr)` sites per kernel. For example, `0fbe46` contains `group_bound = ((1) ? 64 : num_groups)` four times. The g32/g64 pairs differ only in `group_bound`, so making `num_groups` a runtime value would cut `SERVING_PROGRAMS` from 8 to 4 and lift the `PACKED_GROUPS = {32, 64}` limit. Making N and K runtime as well would leave 3 programs.\n- **Inlined helpers.** None of the 76 batch files includes a header:\n  - Each GEMM kernel repeats about 96 identical helper lines (`make_warp_uniform`, `mbarrier_*`, `elect_sync`, `desc_encode`, `tmem_ld_x8`, `tma_4d_gmem2smem`), 3,439 redundant lines in total.\n  - Each binding repeats `EncodeTma_A/B/C_tma` and `CakeSetMaxDynamicSmem`, 5,310 redundant lines in total.\n\n  The grouped bindings share 153 of about 222 lines each (1,797 redundant). `cake_grouped_fp8_fused_silu_quant_host_common.cuh` is byte-identical to `cake_grouped_fp8_gemm_host_common.cuh`, and 4 identical copies exist under `csrc/*/`. The silu `device_common` shares 117 of 139 lines with the grouped one. The estimate is about 11,400 identical-body lines on the current tree, or about 8,700 after the removals below.\n- **Grouped exact shapes.** Despite #5818, `route_for_shape` (`flashinfer/gemm/cake_grouped_fp8_gemm.py` L98-121) still selects 6 programs (7,931 lines) only at exact shapes:\n  - `(4096,2048,4096)` → `f2e4ef`\n  - `(4096,4096,1024)` → `e032da` (128-CTA grid)\n  - `(16384,2048,4096)` → `800df0`\n  - `(16384,4096,1024)` → `28b94c`\n  - `k==128` → `815c90`\n  - `k==384` → `114d58`\n\n  Nearby sizes such as M=4095 or 4097 fall to the generic `deepk_c2` route.\n  - `815c90` is the only program built at a reduced optimization level. It has been since #5500, and neither the source nor the PR text gives a reason.\n  - `917859`, `cd2dca` and `80f755` (2,984 lines) are one kernel in three copies. They differ only in K-group 4 vs 1 (about 18 changed lines) and a scalar-store epilogue (about 42).\n  - The generic `group_gemm_fp8_nt_groupwise_contiguous` never dispatches to Cake; it uses the CuTe-DSL kernel.\n- **mxfp8 dtype copies.** The fp16 program `e5d70aeb` (628 lines) and the bf16 program `8d0f000c` differ in 162 lines. The differences are `#define IS_BF16`, which gates nothing, and 16 widening PTX blocks (L81-257). The bindings differ only in the namespace hash. No library code calls this backend; it is used only by `tests/utils/test_fp8_quantize.py` and `benchmarks/bench_grouped_mxfp8_quantize_backend_comparison.py`.\n- **Dead host code.** `route_for_shape` L115 has a `(m, n, k) == (4096, 2048, 4096)` clause that never runs, because L100 already returns for that shape. Two loaders call `ROUTES.get(arch, ROUTES)` on flat dicts: `flashinfer/jit/gemm/cake_grouped_fp8_gemm.py` L489 and `flashinfer/jit/gemm/cake_grouped_fp8_fused_silu_quant.py` L260. `ROUTE_GEOMETRY['deepk_cg2_ab5_x16_four_load_wait_kg4']` looks unused but is live: `launch_plan` calls `route_geometry` before `resolve_route_for_grid`.\n- **Host path.** Unless `skip_check=True`, each `backend=\"cake\"` batch call runs `_resolve` twice: once in the `backend_requirement` checker (`gemm_base.py` L11508-11512) and again via `run_`/`prepare_batch_deepgemm_fp8_nt_groupwise`. Each run does full tensor validation and evaluates up to 47 predicates. Other per-call work:\n  - Swap routes allocate a workspace with `torch.empty` on every call.\n  - Bindings re-encode 2-5 TMA descriptors and run 20-30 tvm-ffi checks per launch.\n  - Grouped `launch` re-validates and `_rebind()`s 4-6 arguments per call.\n\n  12 helpers (`_require_tensor`, `bind_arguments`, `device_sm_count`, ...) are redefined in 2-3 of the 7 Python modules, about 226 lines.\n- **Other.**\n  - `fused_silu_quant` applies `ROUTE_MODEL_B200` and fixed caps (`CTA_CAP=128`, `SMALL_M_MAX=2048`, `MAX_M=8192`, `ACT_WIDE_MIN_ITEMS=16384`) on every device. All 5 of its programs are reachable.\n  - Entry points reject non-contiguous operands instead of copying them. This is strict rather than wasteful and offers no line reduction.\n  - Formatting is disabled twice: `.clang-format` files with `DisableFormat: true` in the grouped, silu and mxfp8 dirs, and `.pre-commit-config.yaml` L83, which excludes the batch and mxfp8 dirs.\n- **Tests that pin removals.** Each test must be updated in the same PR that removes what it pins.\n  - `tests/gemm/test_cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8_nt_groupwise.py`:\n    - `test_route_chain_covers_inventory_with_registered_programs` only asserts that `select_route` is non-None. On sm_100a, `(4,1024,4096,7168)` resolves only via `seed_swap_m224_packed`.\n    - `test_cuda_graph_capture_of_first_launch` uses a swap route.\n    - `test_packed_ue8m0_scales_match_reference` pins `seed_serving_m32_packed`.\n    - `test_out_of_band_problems_raise` pins the `PACKED_NK`/`PACKED_GROUPS` messages.\n  - `tests/gemm/test_groupwise_scaled_gemm_fp8.py::test_fp8_groupwise_batch_deepgemm_masked[backend=cake]`.\n  - `tests/gemm/test_cake_group_gemm_fp8_nt_groupwise_contiguous.py`:\n    - `test_moe_shapes_match_reference` covers all the exact-shape programs.\n    - `test_unaligned_output_is_written_in_place` pins `80f755`.\n    - `test_launch_plan_below_128_ctas_uses_a_registered_route`.\n    - `test_block_scaled_route_rule` and `test_block_scaled_packed_scales_match_reference`.\n  - `tests/gemm/test_cake_group_gemm_fp8_nt_groupwise_contiguous_silu_quant.py`: `test_launch_plan_matches_prepared` and `test_prepared_matches_torch_reference_chain`.\n  - The four cake cases in `tests/utils/test_fp8_quantize.py`.\n\n  `docs/` has no Cake page for these families, so no doc edits are needed.\n\n**Proposed steps**\n- [ ] Delete the dead host code (−15).\n  - Remove the `route_for_shape` L115 clause, `serving_config` L90-91, the two `ROUTES.get(arch, ROUTES)` lookups and the unused `#define IS_BF16` lines. Keep `ROUTE_GEOMETRY['deepk_cg2_ab5_x16_four_load_wait_kg4']`.\n  - Check with `test_launch_plan_below_128_ctas_uses_a_registered_route`, `test_block_scaled_route_rule` and `test_route_chain_covers_inventory_with_registered_programs`. Add a CPU assertion that `launch_plan`, `select_route` and `serving_config` outputs are unchanged over a shape grid.\n- [ ] Fold the FP16 mxfp8 program into the BF16 source (−~470, or ~600 with one `__device__` widen helper per dtype). Skip this step if the backend is dropped (see the decision step below).\n  - Wrap the 16 widening blocks in `#if IS_BF16 / #else / #endif`.\n  - Compile the single source with `-DIS_BF16={0,1}` under two module names in `flashinfer/jit/cake_grouped_mxfp8_quantize.py`.\n  - Delete `csrc/cake_grouped_mxfp8_quantize/cake_grouped_mxfp8_quantize_e5d70aeb234fb379ed05_{kernel,binding}.cu`, and keep only one of the two formatting opt-outs for the dir.\n  - Validate with per-dtype SASS equality on B200 and GB300, plus the cake cases in `tests/utils/test_fp8_quantize.py`.\n- [ ] Replace the 5,351 rendered route lines in `flashinfer/jit/gemm/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8.py` with a data table and one interpreter (−~4,500).\n  - Each row holds the route, its CTA-interval predicate and exclusions, the program, BLOCK_N, the arg plan and the workspaces.\n  - Share the 29 banded routes between arches, parameterised by SM count and BLOCK_N thresholds.\n  - Drop the two raise stubs and the shadowed swap disjuncts. Drop the `seed_n7168_k2048` blocks only if the swap-AB removal below is not done, because that removal makes them live.\n  - Validate on CPU: old and new `select_route` must agree on route, programs, grids, args and workspaces over `SUPPORTED_NK` × B ∈ {1,2,4,6,8,32,64,128,256} × M ∈ {128,256,512,1024,4096,8192,16384} × every `expected_m` in [0, M], for both tables. Attach the sweep output to the PR.\n- [ ] Move the text-identical helpers into shared headers (−~8,700 after the removals below, ~11,400 if this step goes first).\n  - Device helpers (`mbarrier_*`, `elect_sync`, `tma_4d_gmem2smem`, `tma_store_3d`, tcgen05, `desc_encode`, `tmem_ld_x8`) and the `EncodeTma_A/B/C_tma`/`CakeSetMaxDynamicSmem` host shim go into `csrc/cake_grouped_fp8_gemm/` or a new `csrc/cake_common/`. The remaining kernels and bindings include them (48 of each after the removals).\n  - Delete `cake_grouped_fp8_fused_silu_quant_host_common.cuh` in favour of the byte-identical grouped copy, and merge the 117 shared `device_common` lines.\n  - SASS neutrality has not been measured yet. Confirm byte-identical SASS per JIT module on B200 and GB300, then run `tests/gemm/test_cake_*.py`.\n- [ ] Remove the 5 masked_bn programs (10 files in `csrc/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8/`), their 8 exact routes (~392 Python lines) and the 12 redundant exact routes (456 lines), −6,302 in total.\n  - The 20 affected benchmark points fall through to banded BLOCK_N routes.\n  - Add explicit cases at the 20 points on B200 and GB300, and run `benchmarks/bench_deepgemm_blackwell.py --batch-backend cake` at each. If a point's banded route is slower beyond the benchmark noise band, keep it as a table row.\n- [ ] Remove `eb727f8d280f83b52730` (2 files) and both `seed_large_nk_cta1` blocks (−966).\n  - The `expected_m == 24` points fall to `seed_large_nk_n*_k*` / `seed_n7168_k2048` (`3ac327`).\n  - Confirm with a CPU sweep, saved with the PR, that the `M<=256` clause is never selected on either arch.\n  - Benchmark the 5 `(N,K)` geometries at `expected_m == 24` (B ∈ {1,8,32}, M ∈ {4096,8192,16384}) on B200 and GB300.\n- [ ] Remove swap-AB (12 files, −5,567).\n  - Delete the 6 programs, their 7 route blocks, `ARG_PLANS` plan2/plan4, and the workspace plumbing in `flashinfer/gemm/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8.py` (`WORKSPACE_DTYPES`, `_allocate_workspace`, `_check_workspace`).\n  - Drop the band exclusions on both arches so that those shapes keep a route: sm_100a `n4096_k7168` (1,8192)/(1,16384)/(4,1024) and `n7168_k2048` (1,16384); sm_103a `n4096_k7168` (1,16384) and `n7168_k2048` (1,8192). Also drop the two raise stubs, and keep `seed_n7168_k2048`.\n  - Benchmark the 12 swap shapes on B200 and GB300, comparing cake-banded, cake-swap and deepgemm.\n  - In the same PR, update `test_route_chain_covers_inventory_with_registered_programs` and `test_cuda_graph_capture_of_first_launch`; the latter needs a route that still exists. Confirm that `test_fp8_groupwise_batch_deepgemm_masked[cake]` (group_size=4, m=1024, nk=(4096,7168)) still resolves on sm_100a.\n- [ ] Regenerate the serving kernels in `csrc/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8/` with a runtime `num_groups`, replacing the 4 `group_bound` sites per kernel (−~3,480).\n  - `SERVING_PROGRAMS` drops from 8 to 4 (`n4096_k7168` s5e2/s6e1, `n7168_k2048` s5e2/s5e3), and `PACKED_GROUPS` (`flashinfer/gemm/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8.py` L65) is no longer limited to {32, 64}. Optionally, making `grid_n`, `num_k_blocks` and `sf_packed_cols` runtime as well would leave 3 programs.\n  - SASS-diff the g64 variant; it is expected to be identical except for the `group_bound` load. Benchmark the 8 serving configurations on B200 and GB300.\n  - Update `test_packed_ue8m0_scales_match_reference` and `test_out_of_band_problems_raise`.\n- [ ] Fold the BLOCK_N 9-cluster and 4-cluster in `csrc/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8/` into one parameterised source each, keeping one module per BLOCK_N (−~8,900).\n  - Pass BLOCK_N, TMEM_NCOLS and swizzle as template or `-D` parameters.\n  - Source-level proof is not possible, so each instantiation needs a SASS diff against the current clone on B200 and GB300.\n  - Run the batch tests and `bench_deepgemm_blackwell.py` over the banded routes.\n- [ ] Rework routing in `cake_grouped_fp8_gemm` (−~4,000).\n  - Replace the exact `(m,n,k)` and exact-K routes in `route_for_shape` with bucketed selection (tile geometry + K-group class).\n  - Template the `917859`/`cd2dca`/`80f755` triple, and retire any program that no bucket selects.\n  - Before retiring `815c90`, find out whether its reduced optimization level is intentional: ask the author, or compare SASS and the tp8_down (131072×4096×128) benchmark with and without it.\n  - Benchmark `benchmarks/bench_cake_group_gemm_fp8_nt_groupwise_contiguous.py` at the `test_moe_shapes_match_reference` shapes on B200 and GB300, and update the grouped tests listed above.\n- [ ] Python host path (`flashinfer/gemm/gemm_base.py`, `flashinfer/gemm/cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8.py`): resolve once per `backend=\"cake\"` call instead of twice, and remove the helpers duplicated across the 7 modules (~226 lines). This saving is not included in the estimate below.\n- [ ] Decide whether `mxfp8_grouped_quantize(backend=\"cake\")` should stay. Dropping it removes the 1,296-line `csrc/cake_grouped_mxfp8_quantize/` plus about 424 Python lines (`flashinfer/jit/cake_grouped_mxfp8_quantize.py`, the backend in `flashinfer/quantization/fp8_quantization.py`), instead of the ~470 lines saved by the fold. Compare cake against cutile with `benchmarks/bench_grouped_mxfp8_quantize_backend_comparison.py` on B200 and GB300.\n- [ ] Before the removals, run `test_cake_batch_deepgemm_fp8_nt_groupwise.py` and `test_fp8_groupwise_batch_deepgemm_masked[cake]` on a B200, logging the selected route for each case. This finds fixed-seed cases that currently land on an exact or swap route. It needs a GPU because the CUDA RNG cannot be replayed on CPU.\n\n**Expected size:** about 78.3K lines now, then about 64.6K after the mechanical steps (−13.7K), about 51.8K after the exact-point removals (−12.8K) and about 35.4K after regeneration (−16.4K), for a total reduction of about 42.9K. Helper lines inside removed or folded files were subtracted using per-file averages, so the total can move by ±1.5K depending on step order.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6660/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6660/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6654","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6654/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6654/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6654/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6654","id":5898487358,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABX5PGPg","number":6654,"title":"[cake deslop] blackwell_msa/msa_nvfp4_decode (2): fold uniform-FP8 arch pair, drop xform2 exact-shape routes, template clones (~88K lines)","user":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-17T12:48:51Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T12:48:58Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"parent_issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6682","issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"**Background.** The Cake-generated Blackwell MSA family serves `flashinfer.msa_ops.msa_sparse_attention`, `msa_sparse_decode_attention` and `msa_topk_select` on SM100/SM103 through `flashinfer/msa_ops/_blackwell_sm100.py`. Its 31 dense programs live in `csrc/blackwell_msa`, are registered in `flashinfer/jit/blackwell_msa.py` and are built by AOT. The family also includes the JIT-only NVFP4 sparse-decode backend behind `prepare_msa_nvfp4_sparse_decode` (`flashinfer/experimental/msa_nvfp4_decode`, also exported from `flashinfer.msa_ops`), with 14 programs and 32 (program, arch) specs including sm_107a. At 3184bbea the listed paths hold 157 files / 166,303 lines: `csrc/blackwell_msa` 70,845, `msa_nvfp4_decode` 64,147, Cake Python in `msa_ops` ~8,040, and the 1,866-line loader. The rest is tests, JSON manifests and ~10.3K lines of non-Cake SM12x CuTe-DSL code (`msa_ops/cute_dsl/`, `proxy_score.py`). Round one had two parts. #5778, closed by #5893, merged SM100/SM103 under `__CUDA_ARCH__`, removed the env-var switches and replaced the hash-lock tests, but kept the xform2 exact-shape routes and the `direct_group` alias. #5779, closed by #5875, left one source per NVFP4 program and removed the workspace, but kept the ABI carriers and the 212-SM literal. Several PRs added material back afterwards: #6315 regenerated the family (+11,082/−23,189), #6387 added 8 opt-in `use_cache_hit` union programs (+50,499/−10,185, the largest re-growth), #6397 split `decode_uniform_fp8` back into per-arch programs, and #6410 added `topk_select_keq`/`topk_select_q32` (+10,879/−2,362). #6443, #6459 and #6463 changed FP8 decode, streaming top-k and prefill. All 31 dense programs are routed and all 14 NVFP4 programs are registered, so there are no never-compiled sources.\n\n**Findings**\n- **Per-arch copy re-created by #6397.** `decode_uniform_fp8` is the only remaining pair: `cake_blackwell_msa_a126296d8aaae82459b7` (sm_100a) and `cake_blackwell_msa_2a38253c76cec86dc2cd` (sm_103a). They differ only in `NUM_DECODE_KV_STAGES` (4 vs 8) and its derived SMEM-offset/mbarrier/stage-wrap constants (82 normalized lines). `SMEM_TOTAL` (183296), the block [512,1,1], the flags and the 26-entry arg_plan are identical, and the bindings differ only in the host-shim namespace hash. #6443 regenerated the pair. Both kernels also define `SMEM_SMEM_NVFP4_*` constants and `smem_nvfp4_*` pointers that nothing uses. The other 29 programs are single-source with `__CUDA_ARCH__` guards.\n- **Exact-coordinate routes kept by #5893.** `decode_fp8_q1:{q1_paged_xform2,q1_flat_xform2}` (`0d391c271a9e1dd7de1b`, `2949bc72ee78a52859c9`; 3,390 lines including their registry, dispatcher and test code; present since #4355). `_fp8_q1_schedule` (`_blackwell_sm100.py` l.964-1001) selects them only if all of these hold: `force_fused=True` (the public default is `None`), not under graph capture, causal, bf16 Q / FP8 K, `seqlen_q == 1`, heads 64/4, and exactly paged batch 128 / 4,096 K rows / 32 pages or flat batch 32 / 262,144 K rows. None of the 27 GPU test cases reaches them. Only two bench rows do: `decode_fp8_b128_q1_kv4096_h64` and `coverage_decode_mixed_fp8_b32_q1_kv8192_h64_hkv4_k16_flat`. `decode_m16:bfloat16:float8_e4m3fn:{paged,flat}` serves the same inputs. The module docstring (l.24-25) still says \"no shape-exact routes\".\n- **Dead code and stale docs.**\n  - The sm100a routes `long_bf16_reverse:paged:gqa16:direct_group:*` (`blackwell_msa.py` l.1723-1724, predicate `max_pages == 8192`) point at the same programs as the plain route. They change only the launch-signature string.\n  - Nothing reads the 31 `closure_sha256` fields in `MODULES`: `gen_blackwell_msa_module` uses only `sources`/`compile_flags`, and `aot.py` reads `arches`.\n  - `_PERSISTENT_ABI_CARRIERS`/`_SHORT_ABI_CARRIERS` and the `carriers` branch of `_bind` (`cake_backend.py` l.102-114, l.417-440) match no `ARG_PLANS` name.\n  - `_LONG_WORK_TAIL_SPLIT` and `_LONG_WORK_ORDER_Q_RESIDENT` are always `True`, which leaves a dead `else: work.sort(...)` (l.1658-1659).\n  - `docs/api/sparse.rst` l.106-119 and `CLAUDE.md` l.785-786 still document `FLASHINFER_MSA_PREFILL_SCHEDULE`/`FLASHINFER_MSA_FP8_Q1_SCHEDULE`. #5893 removed both; the unit has no `os.environ`/`getenv` calls.\n- **Duplicated metadata and headers.**\n  - `MODULES` (l.34-1708) is 1,675 of the loader's 1,866 lines. Its 991 `arg_plan` entries are copies of 9 distinct plans (229 entries), so 762 lines are duplicates. One reference per record, as `cake_jit.py` `ARG_PLANS` already does, saves ~730 lines net.\n  - `msa_nvfp4_decode/csrc/cake_device_helpers/` duplicates the shared `csrc/cake_device_helpers/` in 22 local headers (669 lines): 4 are byte-identical, 15 differ only in copyright/SPDX/line wrap, and 3 are missing from the shared directory. `cake_msa_nvfp4_decode_host_common.cuh` (52 lines) is byte-identical to `csrc/blackwell_msa/cake_blackwell_msa_host_common.cuh`.\n  - 16 of the 22 helpers are also copied in `experimental/cake_dsa_train` and `experimental/dense_projection_gemm`. That cross-family dedup is out of scope here.\n- **Dense constant clones.**\n  - `prefill_union`: 12 programs / 29,800 lines. Normalized kernel diffs between variants are 16-224 lines (of ~2,300), and all 12 share one 32-entry arg_plan. `K_scale`/`V_scale` appear only in the kernel signature, yet every binding encodes 5 TMA descriptors, 2 of them for these unused scales, built from `scale_dummy = _eager_dummy(...)` (l.2079).\n  - `decode_m16`: 6 programs / 14,344 lines that differ by 17-583 normalized lines. 23 of the 47 kernel parameters are never read (`Q_prefill`, `K_scale`, `V_scale`, `KV`, `kv_indices`, the `task_*` set, `status`, `msa_split_policy`, …). 6 of the 9 TMA encodes per launch describe dummies built in `_run_decode_m16` (l.2192-2214). #5778 reported this, and it is still there after #5893, #6315, #6397 and #6443.\n  - `long_bf16_reverse`: 5 programs / 8,753 lines. The mains differ by 16-66 lines and the reducers by 3.\n  - `topk_select` vs `topk_select_keq`: an 84-line normalized diff. `_topk_radix_route` (l.284-287) documents them as bit-identical.\n- **NVFP4 split-factor clones.** The 8 union programs (width {2,4} × split {1,2,4,8}, 47,515 lines, added by #6387) run only with `use_cache_hit=True` (`cake_backend.py` l.645). The default `\"auto\"` resolves to False on every shape, and `msa_sparse_decode_attention` never reaches `cake_backend`. The 4 persistent programs (9,722 lines) are one per split factor. #5779 asked for these clones to be templated.\n- **Frozen 212-SM literal.** The sm_107a tail program `21e04eaf` (2,360 lines) is gated on `cluster_capacity {\"212\": 212}` (`tail_plan`, `cake_backend.py` l.190-215). The program is reachable: in-repo comments identify Rubin R200 as the 212-SM sm_107a part (`flashinfer/cake_sampling.py` l.79/l.1214, `flashinfer/gdn_prefill.py` l.632), so it stays. #5779's request to replace the literal with a `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveClusters` query is still open. Other limits baked in at compile time: top-k 16, head_dim 128, page 128, GQA group ≤ 16, and `_UNIFORM_FP8_EVEN_WAVE_GRID = 128`.\n- **Host path and strides.** Work done on every call:\n  - 5-9 TMA encodes per launch (6 dummies in decode, 2 in prefill);\n  - `_launch_signature` built unconditionally (l.2033-2039, l.1999-2010);\n  - `cu_k.zero_()` (l.753, l.2110);\n  - 11 `is_current_stream_capturing` checks;\n  - device-to-host syncs on the long route (l.1183-1185, l.1275-1281, l.1524, l.1632);\n  - the NVFP4 `check_surface` on every dense call (`_try_nvfp4_prefill`/`_try_nvfp4_decode`).\n\n  The NVFP4 runner also re-encodes 5 descriptors per launch. `_validate_attention_tensors` (l.796) requires contiguous operands and rejects packed-KV split views on SM100/103, even though K/V are loaded by TMA (SM120 accepts these views).\n- **Tests.** These tests pin the removals and must be updated in the same PR:\n  - `tests/msa_ops/test_blackwell_msa_routes.py`: the `_derive` branches for `decode_fp8_q1` (l.79-97) and `direct_group` (l.74-78), and `test_fp8_q1_specializations_are_selected_only_on_their_coordinates` (l.114-138).\n  - `tests/jit/test_blackwell_msa_jit.py`: 5 tests over `MODULES`/`ROUTES`. After the uniform-FP8 fold, `test_unbuilt_target_is_rejected` still passes but no longer tests anything.\n  - `tests/experimental/test_cake_msa_nvfp4_decode.py`: the union and split ladder tests (l.425-452, l.479-517), `test_tail_plan_rule`, `test_multi_round_batches_take_the_last_round_split`, and seven names it imports from `cake_backend`. `test_blackwell_msa_topk_dispatch.py` monkeypatches `is_blackwell_msa_device`/`blackwell_msa_topk_select`, so those names must stay.\n  - No GPU test covers `decode_uniform_fp8` (the 27 cases use bf16/fp16 Q only), the xform2 coordinates, or the ≥8192-query long route (all test and bench shapes have q ≤ 4096).\n- **Other bloat and scope.** The two `*_specialized_workloads.json` manifests (143 + 144 lines) hold 3 data rows each. `benchmarks/bench_blackwell_msa_sm100.py` is 1,711 lines (checkout validation, baseline clone, CUPTI, 13 frozen shapes). `csrc/msa_{decode,prefill}_nvfp4_specialized.cu` (2,516 + 1,709 lines) are loaded by this unit's `flashinfer/jit/msa_*_nvfp4_specialized.py` but have no Cake marker. It is unresolved whether they belong in this tracker; the PR that added them (#4982) may say.\n\n**Proposed steps**\n- [ ] Clean up the loader and dispatcher, with no kernel change (−82):\n  - Delete the `direct_group` alias from `ROUTES`, `_blackwell_sm100.py` and `_derive`.\n  - Drop the 31 `closure_sha256` fields, the NVFP4 ABI carriers and the `carriers` branch of `_bind`, the two `_LONG_WORK_*` flags and the dead `else`.\n  - Fix the l.24-25 docstring and remove the env-var/exact-route text from `docs/api/sparse.rst` and `CLAUDE.md`.\n  - Run the routes, jit, topk-dispatch and NVFP4 CPU tests.\n- [ ] Fold `decode_uniform_fp8` into `a126296d` (−1,790):\n  - Put `NUM_DECODE_KV_STAGES` and its derived constants under `#if __CUDA_ARCH__ == 1000` (4 on sm_100a, 8 otherwise), drop the dead `SMEM_SMEM_NVFP4_*`/`smem_nvfp4_*`, and set arches to `[sm_100a, sm_103a]`.\n  - Point `ROUTES` sm103a (l.1755) at the merged program and delete the `2a38253c` kernel, binding and `MODULES` record.\n  - The per-arch SASS diff against today's `a126296d` (sm_100a) and `2a38253c` (sm_103a) has not been run yet; it must be identical.\n  - First add a GPU case for uniform-FP8 paged decode with `force_fused=True` and run it on B200 and B300.\n- [ ] Deduplicate headers (−612):\n  - Switch the 14 NVFP4 bindings to the shared host_common (a one-line include change or a shim).\n  - Move the 3 missing helpers into `csrc/cake_device_helpers/` and delete the 22 local copies. The includes then resolve from `csrc/`.\n  - Confirm with a SASS diff of all 14 NVFP4 programs (sm_100a/sm_103a, plus sm_107a where the toolkit allows) and the NVFP4 suite on B200.\n- [ ] Replace the per-record `arg_plan` copies in `MODULES` with 9 per-route `ARG_PLANS` (−730). Run the CPU tests plus one GPU smoke test per route family.\n- [ ] Remove the two xform2 routes (−3,390):\n  - Delete `cake_blackwell_msa_0d391c271a9e1dd7de1b_*` and `cake_blackwell_msa_2949bc72ee78a52859c9_*`, their `MODULES`/`ROUTES` entries, `_fp8_q1_schedule` and its dispatch branch (l.3029-3052), the non-uniform ternaries in `_run_fp8_direct`, and the two route-test pieces.\n  - Check GPU correctness at both coordinates against the FP32 reference on B200 and B300.\n  - Benchmark the two bench rows with and without the routes. The perf delta has not been measured, and accepting it is a product decision.\n  - Before merging, search downstream MSA integrations (sglang, vLLM, TRT-LLM) for `force_fused=True`.\n- [ ] Replace `cluster_capacity {\"212\": 212}` with a runtime `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveClusters` query (±0). On an R200 (sm_107a) node, confirm that the query returns 212 and that the two tail tests still select `21e04eaf`.\n- [ ] Regenerate the dense family (−38,600):\n  - `prefill_union` 12 → ~4: dtype × mask variants, with layout and GQA group at runtime and no scale dummies.\n  - `decode_m16` 6 → 2: a dtype template, with the 23 dead parameters and 6 dead TMA descriptors removed from the ABI.\n  - `long_bf16_reverse` 3 + 2 → 1 + 1, with group size at runtime.\n  - `topk_select` + `topk_select_keq` → 1.\n  - Require bit-identical outputs on the 27 GPU cases and on new ≥8192-query cases, and keep the 13 bench shapes within noise on B200/B300.\n- [ ] Regenerate the NVFP4 backend (−42,900):\n  - Union 8 → 2 (width 2/4) and persistent 4 → 1 (split factor as a template parameter).\n  - Encode TMA descriptors once at prepare and keep the public `prepare_msa_nvfp4_sparse_decode` signature.\n  - Decide whether the opt-in union family stays at all (dropping it saves 47,515 lines). Check downstream for `use_cache_hit=True` first.\n  - Validate with the device suite, `benchmarks/bench_cake_msa_nvfp4_decode.py` for every (width, split) cell and a CUDA-graph capture test, and rewrite the two ladder tests as behavioural tests.\n- [ ] Trim the host path in `_blackwell_sm100.py` (−40):\n  - Skip `_launch_signature` when workspace is None.\n  - Keep one all-zero `cu_seqlens_k` per device instead of zeroing on every call.\n  - Read the capture state once per call.\n  - Run the NVFP4 `check_surface` hand-off only after a cheap dtype test.\n  - Measure eager host time for decode-flat-bf16-batch148-kv128 on B200 before and after.\n- [ ] Tests and docs (−250 lines, not counting new tests):\n  - Before steps 2, 5 and 7 land, add GPU cases for uniform FP8, FP8-KV q1 at both xform2 coordinates, and causal bf16 prefill with ≥8192-query requests (flat gqa16, paged gqa8/gqa16).\n  - Convert the two `*_workloads.json` files to Python row tuples.\n  - Rewrite the remaining registry-structure tests as behavioural tests.\n  - Trim the checkout/CUPTI scaffolding in the benchmark.\n  - Validate with the `test_msa_nvfp4_{decode,prefill}_sm100.py` allowlist tests; `msa_*_nvfp4_specialized_stats()` output must not change.\n\n**Expected size:** about 166K lines in the listed paths (including ~10.3K lines of non-Cake SM12x code) → about 160K after steps 1-5 (−3.2K mechanical, −3.4K for xform2) → about 78K after the regenerations and the remaining trims (about −88.4K in total).\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6654/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6654/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6600","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6600/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6600/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6600/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6600","id":5890916800,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABXyBBwA","number":6600,"title":"[CAKE] Sampling Kernel Progress Tracker","user":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-16T20:43:12Z","updated_at":"2026-10-16T20:46:34Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"parent_issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254","issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"Parent tracker: #4254\n\nTrack CAKE-generated kernels for **Sampling**.\n\n## Pull requests\n\n### Fused Top-K / Top-P Sampling Pipeline\n\n- #5439\n\n### Architecture Support & Qualification\n\n- #5482\n- #5585\n\n### Kernel & Dispatch Optimizations\n\n- #5607\n- #5636\n- #5684\n- #5731\n- #5847\n- #5950\n- #6065\n- #6105\n- #6143\n\n### Source / Loader Refactoring\n\n- #5985\n\n### CUDA Graph Replay Tests\n\n- #6247\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6600/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6600/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6165","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6165/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6165/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6165/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6165","id":5739637042,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABVhvpMg","number":6165,"title":"[Feature][Rubin] MegaMoE: MXFP8, recomputation, and clamped SwiGLU","user":{"login":"abcdabcd987","id":2470081,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI0NzAwODE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2470081?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987","html_url":"https://github.com/abcdabcd987","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/abcdabcd987/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"hzfan","id":18756967,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NzU2OTY3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18756967?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan","html_url":"https://github.com/hzfan","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":2,"created_at":"2026-10-07T05:00:47Z","updated_at":"2026-10-15T19:12:14Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"hzfan","id":18756967,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE4NzU2OTY3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/18756967?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan","html_url":"https://github.com/hzfan","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hzfan/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"MEMBER","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"parent_issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4254","issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Problem\n\nWe need the MegaMoE training backend in [PR #6053](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/6053) to support activation checkpointing, clamped SwiGLU, and native MXFP8 across Blackwell and Rubin. The target workloads include both 256- and 288-expert models with irregular, unequal per-rank token counts.\n\nThe GB200 benchmark at revision `072340e2` also reports 1.4–1.5× slower steps than MoK under uniform routing, and 58.9 GiB of retained output/context per layer at EP32/64 with overflow-safe schedules; the memory figure excludes symmetric workspaces ([benchmark details](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/6053#issuecomment-6026084025)). These are results for that revision, not Rubin measurements or claims about later revisions.\n\n## Requested outcome\n\n- **Recomputation:** Add a context-only recompute API for activation checkpointing, similar to MoK's `recompute_forward_context`. Rebuild the state needed by backward without rerunning unnecessary down projections, combine, and output epilogues; validate that recomputed and saved-context backward agree.\n\n- **GLM-5.3-Flash support:** Support 288 routed experts, including EP8 with 36 local experts and EP32 with 9. Implement clamped SwiGLU for both routed and shared experts: `silu(clamp(gate, max=10)) * clamp(up, min=-10, max=10)`, with matching backward semantics; retain the unclamped option for the GLM-5.2 workload ([GLM-5.3-Flash implementation](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/8073e6dcaea9aa4b42acf2d92ec72148786bbaeb/src/transformers/models/glm5_next/modeling_glm5_next.py#L86-L142)).\n\n- **MXFP8:** Add native MXFP8 routed-expert forward, input gradients, and weight gradients, not fake quantization followed by BF16 GEMMs. Use a MoK-compatible recipe with caller-prequantized weights and fused activation/gradient quantization, including both operand orientations where needed; keep the shared expert in BF16 initially ([MoK reference API](https://github.com/cursor/mixture-of-kittens/blob/caeb2963f855c7ad53bb50c8bbf211086405cb98/README.md)).\n\n- **Memory and speed:** Address the router-gradient backward overhead, capacity-sized saved routed outputs, and computation on padded source rows identified in the [GB200 benchmark comment](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/6053#issuecomment-6026084025). Reduce retained activation memory without trading away numerical quality, and make performance track real work rather than pessimistic reserved capacity.\n\n## Target hardware\n\nRequired targets are **`sm_100` (B200/GB200), `sm_103` (B300/GB300), and `sm_107` (Rubin)**. Implement and validate the requested capabilities on all three, rather than treating Blackwell as a comparison-only platform.\n\nExpert-parallel groups must support peers spanning multiple hosts within one NVL72 NVLink domain. An inter-rack transport is not required.\n\n## Inference engine\n\nWe use PyTorch native with a custom training integration. This request covers both forward and backward, not an inference-only operator.\n\n## Workload and configuration\n\n- **Data type and quantization:** BF16 baseline and native MXFP8 routed-expert training; BF16 shared expert.\n- **Parallelism:** Expert parallelism within one NVLink domain, combined with expert FSDP and activation checkpointing.\n\n`H` is hidden width and `I` is expert intermediate width. Both workloads use top-8 routing and one shared expert of width `I`.\n\n| Workload | H | I | Routed experts | Target EP sizes | Local routed experts |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---|\n| [GLM-5.2-shaped workload](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/6053#issuecomment-6026084025) | 6144 | 2048 | 256 | 4 / 8 / 16 / 32 / 64 | 64 / 32 / 16 / 8 / 4 |\n| [GLM-5.3-Flash](https://huggingface.co/zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash/raw/main/config.json) | 4096 | 2048 | 288 | 8 / 32 | 36 / 9 |\n\n**The real input-token count `T_r` is irregular, generally not a power of two, and changes across microbatches and runs.** Ranks in the same EP collective can have different counts, including zero; support this independently of uneven received expert loads, without caller-side activation padding.\n\nUse roughly 8K–32K real tokens per rank as the main benchmark range, including counts such as `8191`, `16172`, `16231`, and `32767`. Token counts and routing must remain runtime inputs.\n\n- **Layouts:** Activations are `[T_r,H]`; routing IDs and coefficients are `[T_r,8]`. Gate and up weights are separate tensors, each `[E_local,I,H]`, used as `gate_e = X_e @ W_gate[e].T` and `up_e = X_e @ W_up[e].T`. Down weights are `[E_local,H,I]`, used as `activation_e @ W_down[e].T`.\n\n## Current workaround\n\nThe existing comparison paths are [MoK #17](https://github.com/cursor/mixture-of-kittens/pull/17) and DeepEP HybridEP plus grouped GEMMs, as described in the [benchmark setup](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/6053#issuecomment-6026084025). For the measured adapter revision, activation checkpointing replays the full forward because it lacks a context-only entry point ([recompute comparison](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/6053#issuecomment-6026084025)).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- **Hardware coverage:** Run correctness and performance qualification on `sm_100`, `sm_103`, and `sm_107` for the listed workloads.\n- **Training correctness:** Validate outputs, input gradients, routing-coefficient gradients, and all expert/shared weight gradients against independent references. Include clamp boundaries, uneven/empty ranks, skewed routing, and recomputation; support FP32 weight-gradient accumulation and document the BF16/MXFP8 numerical behavior.\n- **Performance:** Compare complete forward/backward and checkpointed execution against MoK and HybridEP plus grouped GEMMs. Include scheduling, quantization, copies, and replay, and report total peak memory with matched ring sizes or an explicit latency-versus-memory comparison.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6165/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6165/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6409","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6409/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6409/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6409/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6409","id":5825805319,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABWz68Bw","number":6409,"title":"docs: track API, prose, HTML rendering, and broken-link findings","user":{"login":"kangbintNV","id":191459121,"node_id":"U_kgDOC2lvMQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/191459121?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV","html_url":"https://github.com/kangbintNV","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"kangbintNV","id":191459121,"node_id":"U_kgDOC2lvMQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/191459121?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV","html_url":"https://github.com/kangbintNV","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-13T06:42:16Z","updated_at":"2026-10-13T06:44:02Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"kangbintNV","id":191459121,"node_id":"U_kgDOC2lvMQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/191459121?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV","html_url":"https://github.com/kangbintNV","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kangbintNV/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Scope\n\nDocumentation findings span API behavior, technical accuracy, prose, HTML rendering, and links. The examples below show one case for each issue type; all four identified broken links are listed. Source corrections are in PR #5494. After the docs are published, verify the rendered pages and link destinations.\n\n## One example per issue type\n\n- **API Contract (P0):** The [cascade API](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/api/cascade.html) labels the `BatchPrefillWithSharedPrefixPagedKVCacheWrapper` constructor as `BatchDecodeWithSharedPrefixPagedKVCacheWrapper`.\n- **Technical Accuracy (P1):** The [JAX FFI tutorial](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/tutorials/generated/jax_tvm_ffi/flashinfer_jax_tvm_ffi.html) says three additional packages are required but lists only two.\n- **Grammar (P1):** The [attention API](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/api/attention.html) says `for batch of requests`; it needs `for a batch of requests`.\n- **Clarity (P1):** The [sampling API](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/api/sampling.html) repeats `the sampling` in one sentence about deterministic sampling.\n- **Punctuation (P1):** The [sampling API](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/api/sampling.html) joins two independent sentences with a comma after `probabilities`.\n- **Spelling (P1):** The [communication API](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/api/comm.html) says `Initialization referece`.\n- **Terminology (P2):** The [KV layout tutorial](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/tutorials/kv_layout.html) says `kV-Cache` instead of `KV-Cache`.\n- **Formatting / HTML rendering (P0):** The [sampling API](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/api/sampling.html) displays literal `:param logits:` and `:type logits:` inside the `sampling_from_logits` description instead of structured parameter fields.\n\n## Broken Link (P0): all four cases\n\n1. **CUDA Programmatic Dependent Launch, published API:** The [attention API](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/api/attention.html) uses an [old URL](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-c-programming-guide/#programmatic-dependent-launch-and-synchronization) that returns 404. Use the [current CUDA section](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/04-special-topics/programmatic-dependent-launch.html).\n2. **FlashInfer MLA article, published API:** The [attention API](https://docs.flashinfer.ai/api/attention.html) links to a [missing article](http://flashinfer.ai/2025/02/10/flashinfer-deepseek-mla.html). Use the [FlashInfer 0.2 MLA section](https://flashinfer.ai/2024/12/16/flashinfer-v02-release.html#fused-multi-head-latent-attention-mla-decoding-kernel).\n3. **KDA repository, source documentation:** [`docs/api/kda.rst`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/b18a18247a831ddefa5a8308463c1113ac4b508a/docs/api/kda.rst#L650) links to an [unavailable upstream repository](https://github.com/humanfia/kda-for-kda-release). Link to the [vendored KDA implementation](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/tree/main/flashinfer/kda_kernels/persistent).\n4. **CUDA Binary Compatibility, source documentation:** [`docs/design_docs/jit_cache_provider_wheels.md`](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/b18a18247a831ddefa5a8308463c1113ac4b508a/docs/design_docs/jit_cache_provider_wheels.md#L250) uses an [old section URL](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-c-programming-guide/index.html#binary-compatibility) that redirects to the guide index without the intended anchor. Use the [current Binary Compatibility section](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/01-introduction/cuda-platform.html#binary-compatibility).\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6409/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6409/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6235","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6235/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6235/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6235/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6235","id":5757805807,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABVzEk7w","number":6235,"title":"[Bug][v0.7.2rc1] tests/utils/test_cake_sampling.py AssertionError: [] assert ([])","user":{"login":"nvamyt","id":223233723,"node_id":"U_kgDODU5Guw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/223233723?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt","html_url":"https://github.com/nvamyt","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nvamyt/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11647775379,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACtkLykw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/ci:%20health","name":"ci: health","color":"b60205","default":false,"description":"Impacts healthiness of CI tests","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":2,"created_at":"2026-10-08T07:28:43Z","updated_at":"2026-10-08T09:46:21Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"yyihuang","id":52445717,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUyNDQ1NzE3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52445717?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang","html_url":"https://github.com/yyihuang","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yyihuang/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"<!-- dlqa-triage:flashinfer-ci:v1 -->\n<!-- dlqa-triage:flashinfer-ci-request:fbd8a233f48698e638e3402217cbc3b92389653d27dc9be96fd6fcdc338260ea -->\n\n## Summary\nFlashInfer 0.7.2rc1 (4d4a0a2f) CI reports a test assertion affecting 1 saved failure occurrence(s) across 1 affected test(s). The saved evidence covers GB300 / cu129 in flashinfer-ci.\n\n## CI environment\n\n- Commit: 4d4a0a2f\n- Branch: release-v0.7.2\n- Pipeline: [#72214613](https://nv/flashinfer-ci/-/pipelines/72214613)\n- Affected scope: 1 saved failure occurrence(s) across 1 affected test(s)\n- Affected tests:\n  - tests/utils/test_cake_sampling.py::test_cuda_graph_capture_never_registers_the_generator\n- Failed jobs:\n  - [unit_test_gb300: [0, cu129]](https://nv/flashinfer-ci/-/jobs/474389289)\n\n## Failure\n\n```text\nAssertionError: [] assert ([])\n```\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```bash\npytest 'tests/utils/test_cake_sampling.py::test_cuda_graph_capture_never_registers_the_generator'\n```\n\nGenerated from CI test identity; not independently verified.\n\n## Case History\n- 3 confirmed failure pipeline(s); first seen 2026-10-06, last seen 2026-10-08.\n- 2026-10-08 — 0.7.2rc1 (4d4a0a2f) — GB300 / cu129 — [pipeline #72214613](https://nv/flashinfer-ci/-/pipelines/72214613) / [job](https://nv/flashinfer-ci/-/jobs/474389289)\n- 2026-10-07 — 0.7.2 (aa651045) — GB300 / cu129 — [pipeline #72125911](https://nv/flashinfer-ci/-/pipelines/72125911) / [job](https://nv/flashinfer-ci/-/jobs/473732770)\n- 2026-10-06 — 0.7.1 (d8940515) — GB300 / cu129 — [pipeline #71876188](https://nv/flashinfer-ci/-/pipelines/71876188) / [job](https://nv/flashinfer-ci/-/jobs/471694608)\n\n## Related PR\n#6065 ","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6235/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6235/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6112","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6112/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6112/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6112/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6112","id":5725320188,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABVUFz_A","number":6112,"title":"[Perf][GB300] mm_fp4 CuTe-DSL autotuning selects inconsistent tactics across fresh processes","user":{"login":"yufeiwu-nv","id":230315618,"node_id":"U_kgDODbpWYg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/230315618?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv","html_url":"https://github.com/yufeiwu-nv","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/yufeiwu-nv/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-10-06T08:23:40Z","updated_at":"2026-10-06T08:23:53Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"On one GB300, fresh-process autotuning of the same NVFP4 `mm_fp4` case selects three different CuTe-DSL tactics, producing a **15.41% spread** in per-process median latency. Increasing only the tuning sample budget from 10 to 50 reduces that spread to **1.37%** in this experiment. All reference checks pass; this is a performance-stability report.\n\n**Environment and case**\n\n- FlashInfer `0ee987a6bb67883ca64cdee52f2f908eb882fecc` (0.7.0), tested September 30, 2026.\n- NVIDIA GB300 (SM103); driver 580.159.03; PyTorch 2.13.0+cu130, CUDA 13.0.\n- nvidia-cutlass-dsl 4.7.0; cupti-python 13.4.0; nvidia-cuda-cupti 13.4.92; cuda-python 13.0.0.\n- Other backends in the same benchmark: cuDNN 9.27.0.42, cudnn-frontend 1.27.0, cuBLAS 13.8.1.7.\n- `mm_fp4`, backend `cute-dsl` with `--autotune`, M=16, N=512, K=7168, NVFP4, BF16 output, 128x4 scale layout; case tag `DeepSeek-R1`.\n\n**Controlled comparison**\n\nSame physical GPU, ten fresh processes per arm, alternating A/B and B/A order. An initial compilation/warmup process was discarded. JIT artifacts were reused, but tuned winners were not persisted across processes. Shape, seed, backend list, reference checking, and final measurement settings were identical: CUPTI + CUDA graph + cold L2, 5 warmups and 30 measured iterations.\n\nThe baseline used the default `autotune()` settings. The trial used an outer `autotune(False, cuda_graph_profile_replays=5)` scope; the benchmark's own nested `autotune(True)` scopes inherited the override. In this FP4 cold-L2 graph path, that changes actual samples per candidate from 10 to 50, with an L2 flush before every sample. The override applied to the requested backends' tuning scopes; the table below is the CuTe-DSL result. Neither arm forced a tactic. Identical diagnostic hooks recorded choices and effective sample counts outside the timed GPU regions.\n\n| Metric across 10 process medians | Default: 10 tuning samples | Trial: 50 tuning samples |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Median latency | 4.944 us | 4.712 us |\n| Minimum–maximum | 4.672–5.392 us | 4.688–4.752 us |\n| Spread, `(max/min - 1) * 100` | 15.41% | 1.37% |\n| Population CV, `std/mean * 100` | 5.69% | 0.45% |\n\nSelected tactic tuples and counts:\n\n| Tactic | Default | Trial |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `((128, 8), (1, 1), True, False, 'sm100sk', 2)` | 4/10 | 0/10 |\n| `((128, 16), (1, 1), True, False, 'sm100sk', 4)` | 1/10 | 0/10 |\n| `((128, 8), (1, 1), True, False, 'sm100sk', 4)` | 5/10 | 10/10 |\n\nThe slower default outcomes recur late in the interleaved sequence, not only during initial warmup. Individual-run relative standard deviation remains around 3% in both arms; the improvement shown here concerns fresh-process retuning. The whole benchmark section, including tuning all four supported backends, takes a median 3.32 s versus 5.19 s; this is not kernel latency.\n\n**Reproduction**\n\nInstall the tested commit and its dependencies, select one GB300 with `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES`, and run from the repository root. Save this simplified wrapper as `repro_fp4_autotune.py`; it runs the original benchmark and records selected CuTe-DSL tactics:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport runpy\nimport sys\nfrom flashinfer.autotuner import AutoTuner, autotune\n\nsamples = int(sys.argv[1])\nassert samples in (10, 50)\nsys.path.insert(0, 'benchmarks')\nchoices = set()\noriginal_choose = AutoTuner.choose_one\n\ndef record_choice(self, custom_op, runners, tuning_config, inputs, **kwargs):\n    runner, tactic = original_choose(self, custom_op, runners, tuning_config, inputs, **kwargs)\n    if type(runner).__name__ == 'CuteDSLFp4GemmRunner' and tactic != -1:\n        choices.add(repr(tactic))\n    return runner, tactic\n\nAutoTuner.choose_one = record_choice\nsys.argv = ['benchmarks/flashinfer_benchmark.py',\n    '--routine', 'mm_fp4', '--m', '16', '--n', '512', '--k', '7168',\n    '--out_dtype', 'bfloat16',\n    '--backends', 'cudnn', 'cutlass', 'cute-dsl', 'trtllm', 'b12x',\n    '--use_128x4_sf_layout', '--use_nvfp4', '--autotune', '--refcheck',\n    '--random_seed', '42', '--num_iters', '30', '--dry_run_iters', '5',\n    '-vv', '--generate_repro_command', '--case_tag', 'DeepSeek-R1',\n    '--output_path', f'fp4-{samples}-{os.getpid()}.csv']\noptions = {} if samples == 10 else {'cuda_graph_profile_replays': 5}\ntry:\n    with autotune(False, **options):\n        runpy.run_path('benchmarks/flashinfer_benchmark.py', run_name='__main__')\nfinally:\n    AutoTuner.choose_one = original_choose\nprint('CuTe-DSL selected tactics:', sorted(choices))\n```\n\n```bash\nexport CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0\n# Discard compilation/warmup result.\npython3 repro_fp4_autotune.py 10\nfor i in $(seq 0 9); do\n  if (( i % 2 == 0 )); then arms=\"10 50\"; else arms=\"50 10\"; fi\n  for samples in $arms; do\n    python3 repro_fp4_autotune.py \"$samples\"\n  done\ndone\n```\n\n**Expected behavior and scope**\n\nDefault autotuning should avoid repeatedly choosing materially slower tactics for an identical workload. Exact tactic identity need not be deterministic when candidates have equivalent execution performance. Please investigate sampling/ranking robustness or a validation step for close candidates while retaining automatic selection.\n\nThe 50-sample arm is diagnostic evidence, not a claim that 50 is the required/default budget or a universally sufficient fix. This is one shape on one GPU, not a correctness failure or a bisected regression; the introducing commit is unknown. This report does not claim a retest of the current main branch.\n\nRelated: #3920 discusses autotuning measurement stability; #3648 is a closed CuTe-DSL FP4 selection report with different shapes/tactics. #4433 and its fix #4459 concern the separate cuTile selection path; this report does not establish the same root cause or a regression of that fix.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6112/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/6112/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5355","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5355/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5355/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5355/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5355","id":5519757050,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABSQDO-g","number":5355,"title":"JIT `topk` module ignores `FLASHINFER_CUDA_ARCH_LIST`: hardcoded `sm89_nvcc_flags` produce an sm_89-only binary","user":{"login":"jhcdlg","id":126441103,"node_id":"U_kgDOB4lWjw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/126441103?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg","html_url":"https://github.com/jhcdlg","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jhcdlg/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":10086440798,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACWTLfXg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/op:%20misc","name":"op: misc","color":"29baf5","default":false,"description":"norm, activation, sampling, RoPE, quantization, etc.","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"XFDG","id":91861184,"node_id":"U_kgDOBXmwwA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/91861184?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG","html_url":"https://github.com/XFDG","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":7,"created_at":"2026-09-20T18:46:05Z","updated_at":"2026-10-06T01:08:00Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"XFDG","id":91861184,"node_id":"U_kgDOBXmwwA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/91861184?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG","html_url":"https://github.com/XFDG","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/XFDG/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"> Filing against flashinfer (this is where the root cause is). We hit it through SGLang,\n> which is why the reproduction below mentions SGLang  -  but the mechanism is entirely in\n> `flashinfer/jit/`.\n\n## Environment\n\n- `flashinfer` **0.6.18** (installed via SGLang `main`, HEAD `5e9342d`, 2026-09-18)\n- Two GPUs in one host, TP=2: **RTX 3080 (sm_86)** + RTX 4080 SUPER (sm_89)\n- CUDA 13.0, torch 2.13.0+cu130, Python 3.11.16\n- flashinfer JIT nvcc: **13.4 (V13.4.59, from the pip `nvidia/cu13` package)**  -  i.e. *not*\n  the 13.0 that `torch.version.cuda` reports. The build metadata baked into the artifacts\n  scanned below reads `Cuda compilation tools, 13.4, V13.4.59`.\n- `FLASHINFER_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=\"8.6 8.9\"` was **set** in the environment\n- `FLASHINFER_EXTRA_CUDAFLAGS` was also tried, with `-gencode` for both 86 and 89  -  **no effect** on this issue\n\n## Summary\n\nThe JIT-compiled `topk` module ships the hardcoded flag list `sm89_nvcc_flags`\n(`flashinfer/jit/core.py`  -  line 123 in 0.6.18, which is the version we hit this on; the same\nlist is at line 156 on current `main`), which contains an explicit\n`-gencode=arch=compute_89,code=sm_89`. `flashinfer/jit/cpp_ext.py:216` (same line in 0.6.18 and\non `main`) then decides that\n**a module which already carries its own `-gencode` should not get the architecture list\nappended**  -  so `FLASHINFER_CUDA_ARCH_LIST` is silently ignored for that module only.\n\nResult: `topk.so` is built for sm_89 alone. On any other architecture it fails at **run\ntime**, not build time  -  and the failure surfaces on the *first real inference request*,\nwhile the server looks perfectly healthy.\n\n## Symptom (this part is what makes it expensive to diagnose)\n\n- The server **starts normally**: `Uvicorn running`, `/v1/models` returns 200, weights\n  loaded, no warning anywhere in the log.\n- **Every real inference request fails.** `num_running_reqs` and `kv_used_tokens` stay **0**\n  the whole time  -  the request never reaches the scheduler, so the first thing anyone\n  suspects is the frontend / networking, not a kernel.\n- The log accumulates (18 occurrences in our run):\n\n```\nRuntimeError: Check failed: (status == cudaSuccess) is false:\nTopK failed with error code no kernel image is available for execution on the device\n```\n\n- A handful of other ops (`BatchPrefillWithPagedKVCache`) fail the same way, which is what\n  made us first chase the *general* `FLASHINFER_CUDA_ARCH_LIST` question instead of\n  looking at a per-module override.\n\n## Where the message comes from\n\n```\nflashinfer/data/csrc/topk.cu:84:\n    TVM_FFI_ICHECK(status == cudaSuccess)\n      << \"TopK failed with error code \" << cudaGetErrorString(status);\n```\n\nNot torch: `torch.topk` works fine on sm_86 here  -  we tested 6 variants (different dtypes\nand k values) on that GPU, all OK. So it is specifically flashinfer's JIT `topk` module.\n\n## Decisive evidence: scan the architectures actually compiled into each JIT artifact\n\nScanning every `.so` under `~/.cache/sglang/.cache/flashinfer/0.6.18/86_89/cached_ops/`:\n\n| Artifact | Architectures present | sm_86 usable? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `sampling/sampling.so` | **86, 89** | yes |\n| `batch_prefill_with_kv_cache.../*.so` | **86, 89** | yes |\n| **`topk/topk.so`** | **89 only** | no |\n\nEverything else in the same cache directory got both architectures. Only `topk` did not.\n\n## Root cause\n\n```python\n# flashinfer/jit/core.py:123   -  a hardcoded per-architecture flag list\nsm89_nvcc_flags = [\"-gencode=arch=compute_89,code=sm_89\", \"-DFLASHINFER_ENABLE_FP8_E8M0\"]\n```\n\n```python\n# flashinfer/jit/cpp_ext.py:216   -  if the module brings its own -gencode, the arch list is not appended\nmodule_has_gencode = any(flag.startswith(\"-gencode=\") for flag in extra_cuda_cflags)\n```\n\n`topk` is one of the modules that carries `sm89_nvcc_flags`, so it hits both: the module\ndeclares sm_89 explicitly, and the general mechanism declines to add anything else.\n`FLASHINFER_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=\"8.6 8.9\"` therefore has no effect on this module.\n\nBecause the user-facing env var is documented as *the* way to control target\narchitectures, this failure mode is very hard to reason about from the outside: the same env\nvar works for `sampling` and `batch_prefill` in the very same build.\n\n## Suggested fix direction\n\n- Generate these per-architecture flag lists **from** the effective arch list instead of\n  hardcoding a single `-gencode`, or\n- In `cpp_ext.py`, treat a module's own `-gencode` as **additive** (append the arch list\n  rather than skipping it), or at minimum warn when the two disagree  -  right now the\n  mismatch is completely silent.\n- A warning when the compiled artifact's architecture set does not cover the GPUs actually\n  present would have saved us a lot of time.\n\n## Impact / who else hits this\n\nAnyone running flashinfer **0.6.18** on a GPU that is not sm_89  -  e.g. sm_86 (RTX 30-series),\nsm_80 (A100), sm_75  -  **for the `topk` op path**. We only verified sm_86, and only on one\nhost, so we do not want to overstate the breadth; but the mechanism is architecture-list\nlogic, not hardware-specific.\n\nNote that on our machine **a single-GPU run using only the 4080 SUPER (sm_89) works fine**  - \nthe failure needs a non-sm_89 device to be part of the run. So a mixed-architecture host\nfails on every request, while the same software on a single sm_89 card is completely healthy.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- Verified on one host, two architectures (sm_86 + sm_89), one flashinfer version (0.6.18).\n- We did not bisect which flashinfer release introduced `sm89_nvcc_flags` for `topk`.\n- Our evidence for \"the arch list is ignored\" is the built artifact's architecture set plus\n  the source lines above  -  we did not instrument the JIT build to print the final nvcc\n  command line. If that would help, we are happy to re-run with more logging.\n\n## Workaround we are using\n\nRemove the non-sm_89 GPU from the run (i.e. use a single sm_89 card), which sidesteps the\n`topk` module entirely. Not viable for us long-term since our workload needs TP across both\ncards, so we would really appreciate a fix rather than a workaround.\n\n## Related reports we checked\n\n- **#5237** (*open*)  -  *fix(jit): allow SM121 (DGX Spark GB10) in the moe_utils JIT arch\n  filter*. Same class of problem (a JIT arch filter that excludes an architecture), but the\n  opposite direction from ours: there an arch is filtered out, whereas here a module-level\n  `-gencode` causes the global arch list to be skipped  -  silently, with no warning.\n- Note that issue **#38515** is on the SGLang side and concerns per-op cubin gaps in\n  `sgl_kernel`; we hit that as well and are commenting there separately. It is a different bug\n  from this one.\n\nWe did not find a report of the `sm89_nvcc_flags` / `module_has_gencode` interaction. If it is\nalready tracked somewhere, we are happy to move this write-up there instead.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5355/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5355/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/2896","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/2896/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/2896/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/2896/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/2896","id":4140613641,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM72zMAJ","number":2896,"title":"[Bug] Tensor-core decode path produces degenerate output for GQA group_size=7 in bf16 due to precision loss","user":{"login":"Kare0638","id":201742633,"node_id":"U_kgDODAZZKQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/201742633?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638","html_url":"https://github.com/Kare0638","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Kare0638/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":6,"created_at":"2026-03-26T04:59:19Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T15:36:06Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Summary\n\n`BatchDecodeWithPagedKVCacheWrapper` with `use_tensor_cores=True` rounds the attention probability matrix P to bf16 for the P×V GEMM, causing precision loss that accumulates over autoregressive decoding. For GQA models with `group_size=7` (e.g., Qwen2.5-Math-7B), this makes `temperature=1.0` generation produce garbage output. The non-tensor-core decode kernel cannot be used as a fallback because it crashes with `Unsupported group_size: 7`. This directly blocks RL training (e.g., GRPO) which requires `temperature=1.0` as a fundamental algorithmic requirement for on-policy sampling.\n\n## Environment\n\n| Component | Version |\n|-----------|---------|\n| GPU | NVIDIA RTX 3090 (24GB), SM 8.6 |\n| CUDA | 12.3 |\n| FlashInfer | 0.6.3 |\n| PyTorch | 2.9.1 |\n| OS | Ubuntu 22.04 |\n| Python | 3.10 |\n\nAlso reproduced on RTX A6000 (SM 8.6) by the original reporter.\n\n## Bug Description\n\n`BatchDecodeWithPagedKVCacheWrapper` with `use_tensor_cores=True` produces degenerate output (garbage tokens, answering wrong questions) for models with GQA `group_size=7` (e.g., Qwen2.5-Math-7B: 28 query heads, 4 KV heads) in bf16 at `temperature=1.0`.\n\nThe non-tensor-core decode path (`use_tensor_cores=False`) is not available for `group_size=7`:\n\n```\nbatch_decode.cu:63: Unsupported group_size: 7\n```\n\nAs noted in #703, the tensor-core decode path routes through the prefill kernel, which **rounds the attention probability matrix P to bf16** for the P×V GEMM. The non-tensor-core decode kernel uses FP32 internally. This bf16 rounding causes precision loss that compounds over autoregressive decoding steps.\n\n**The bug is model and temperature sensitive:**\n\n| Condition | Result |\n|-----------|--------|\n| Qwen2.5-Math-7B (GQA=7), temp=1.0, FlashInfer decode | **Degenerate** |\n| Qwen2.5-Math-7B (GQA=7), temp=1.0, Triton decode (FP32 accum) | Correct |\n| Qwen2.5-Math-7B (GQA=7), temp=0.5, FlashInfer decode | Correct |\n| Qwen2.5-Math-7B (GQA=7), temp=1.0, FlashInfer decode, disable CUDA graph | **Still degenerate** |\n| Qwen2.5-Math-7B (GQA=7), temp=1.0, FlashInfer decode, `use_tensor_cores=False` | **Crash: Unsupported group_size: 7** |\n\n## To Reproduce\n\nMinimal reproduction using SGLang (the downstream caller):\n\n```python\n# Install: pip install \"sglang[all]==0.5.9\" openai\n\n# 1. Start server with FlashInfer decode (default) - DEGENERATE\n# python -m sglang.launch_server --model-path Qwen/Qwen2.5-Math-7B --port 30000\n\n# 2. Start server with Triton decode - CORRECT\n# python -m sglang.launch_server --model-path Qwen/Qwen2.5-Math-7B --port 30000 --attention-backend triton\n\nimport openai\nclient = openai.Client(base_url=\"http://127.0.0.1:30000/v1\", api_key=\"none\")\nresp = client.completions.create(\n    model=\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-Math-7B\",\n    prompt=\"Convert the point $(0,3)$ in rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates. \"\n           \"Enter your answer in the form $(r,\\\\theta),$ where $r > 0$ and \"\n           \"$0 \\\\le \\\\theta < 2 \\\\pi.$ Let's think step by step\\n\",\n    max_tokens=512,\n    temperature=1.0,\n    top_p=1.0,\n)\nprint(resp.choices[0].text)\n```\n\n**FlashInfer decode output (degenerate):**\n```\nFirst tell me what does \"r\" equal??\nOptions :0,3|-3,0|np|total of two points.\n_USA said 3 Let's break this problem mentally...\nDo both sides make up the opposite side ($(3,0)=y0+y1800000000000000$)+3answer?$\n（followed by garbage tokens and random numbers）\n```\n\n**Triton decode output (correct):**\n```\n$y = 3$\n$\\sqrt {0^2 + 3^2} = r$ then $r = 3$\nand\n$\\theta \\in  \\frac {\\pi}{2}, \\frac {3\\pi}{2}$\n$\\theta = \\frac {\\pi}{2}$\n```\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe tensor-core decode path should produce output with comparable quality to the non-tensor-core path (or Triton's FP32 accumulation path). Either:\n\n1. The tensor-core decode path should use FP32 accumulation for the P×V GEMM (instead of rounding P to bf16), or\n2. The non-tensor-core decode kernel should support `group_size=7` (and other non-power-of-2 sizes), so users can opt out of the tensor-core path when precision matters.\n\n## Impact\n\nThis bug blocks GRPO (Group Relative Policy Optimization) training on Qwen2.5-Math-7B using SGLang as the rollout engine. GRPO requires `temperature=1.0` for on-policy sampling to generate diverse trajectories — this is not a tunable parameter but a fundamental requirement of the RL algorithm. Any GQA model with `group_size` not in {1, 2, 4, 8} and a flat logit distribution is potentially affected.\n\n## Related\n\n- #703 — Confirms tensor-core path rounds P to fp16/bf16 for P×V GEMM\n- sgl-project/sglang#21238 — Downstream bug report with full reproduction details and experiment log\n\ncc @yzh119\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/2896/reactions","total_count":2,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/2896/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5637","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5637/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5637/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5637/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5637","id":5611994266,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABToA8mg","number":5637,"title":"[Bug] chain_speculative_sampling reuses a Philox draw across consecutive B=1, N=3 calls","user":{"login":"cudabinary","id":334908806,"node_id":"U_kgDOE_ZNhg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/334908806?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary","html_url":"https://github.com/cudabinary","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/cudabinary/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":5761844621,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABV27FjQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/bug","name":"bug","color":"d73a4a","default":true,"description":"Something isn't working","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-09-28T08:59:46Z","updated_at":"2026-09-28T08:59:57Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Before submitting\n\n- [x] I have searched existing issues and did not find a solution or an existing report.\n\n### Environment\n\n```text\nObserved in a retained CUDA run on 2026-09-28:\nFlashInfer: 0.7.0, source-built at c0aca1fa944cd5efd0e7bf5c003dd77e35d341d1\nGPU: NVIDIA Tesla T4, compute capability 7.5\nPython: 3.13.15, GCC 13.3.0\nPyTorch: 2.11.0+cu128\ntorch.version.cuda: 12.8\nBackend: FlashInfer native CUDA ChainSpeculativeSampling, deterministic=True\nDispatch: batch=1, num_speculative_tokens=3, vocabulary=3; float32 probabilities, int32 draft IDs\nBuild: source-built release JIT, FLASHINFER_JIT_DEBUG=0\n\nThe original run did not capture python -m flashinfer.collect_env output.\nThe information above is preserved run metadata, not output from that command.\nDriver and actually-loaded cuDNN/cuBLAS versions are not asserted here.\nNo new GPU run was performed while preparing this report.\n```\n\n### Bug description\n\nWith one private CUDA `torch.Generator` continued across two calls to `chain_speculative_sampling`, I observed reuse of the first call's recovery draw as the next call's first acceptance draw. The concrete case is **B=1, N=3 in both calls, vocabulary=3, float32, deterministic=True**. The generator is initialized once per two-call program and is not reset between its calls.\n\nThe native observations were made at commit `c0aca1fa944cd5efd0e7bf5c003dd77e35d341d1`. The overlapping random-word ranges arise in the generator-managed path, without the caller supplying seed or offset.\n\n### Source mechanism\n\n- The [generator helper](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/c0aca1fa944cd5efd0e7bf5c003dd77e35d341d1/flashinfer/sampling.py#L49-L65) advances the offset and returns the **advanced** value. The [chain wrapper](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/c0aca1fa944cd5efd0e7bf5c003dd77e35d341d1/flashinfer/sampling.py#L722-L745) requests `B*(N+1)`, rounded to a multiple of four. The two managed launches therefore start at 4 and 8.\n- In the [kernel](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/blob/c0aca1fa944cd5efd0e7bf5c003dd77e35d341d1/include/flashinfer/sampling.cuh#L1882-L1964), rejection at zero-based position j consumes j+1 prefix checks, N-j diagnostic redraws (starting at j again), and one recovery draw: **N+2 scalar words** per local stream. If all proposals pass, consumption is **N+1**, including the bonus.\n- Thus a forced first rejection at N=3 consumes coordinates 4,5,6,7,8; recovery uses 8. The next call starts at 8 and uses that same coordinate for its first acceptance.\n\n```\nmanaged:       call 0 [4 5 6 7 8(R)]   call 1 [8(A) 9 10 11 (12)]\nexplicit 4/12: call 0 [4 5 6 7 8(R)]   call 1 [12(A) 13 14 15 (16)]\n```\n\nThe parenthesized fifth cell of call 1 is consumed only on rejection. This is a source-derived coordinate diagram, not timing data.\n\n### Observable effect and expected behavior\n\nUse proposal q=(1,0,0), so draft IDs [0,0,0] are valid without additional proposal RNG. In call 0, the first target row is (0,1/2,1/2), forcing recovery; its remaining rows and all rows of call 1 are (1/2,1/2,0). Decode `bR = (call0_token0 == 1)` and `bA = (call1_token0 == 0)`.\n\nThese two bits expose the same half-threshold predicate at the reused coordinate. Under the independent-coordinate reference needed by this small consumer, their equality event E has probability 1/2. In the retained 208-seed paired study, E occurred **208/208 with managed offsets**, **208/208 with explicit 4/8 replay**, and **97/208 with explicit same-start disjoint allocation**. The first two complete returned token arrays and both counters match between managed and overlap replay at all 208 positions.\n\nI expect generator-managed allocation to avoid this overlap between output-determining recovery and acceptance decisions. The observed 208/208 is this event count in this construction, not a claim that every sampler output is wrong.\n\nThe script below is a newly extracted minimal reproducer. Its exact two-call inputs and default seeds have retained native records, but **this standalone script has only been syntax-checked, not separately run on a GPU**. The default seeds are the first eight panel positions in order, without outcome selection. It reports offsets, full returns and counters; it is not a benchmark or a new significance test.\n\n\n### Minimal reproduction\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Proposed minimal CUDA reproducer; NOT GPU-executed as this standalone file.\n\nTargets flashinfer commit c0aca1fa944cd5efd0e7bf5c003dd77e35d341d1.\nThe identical first-two-call inputs/seeds were already executed in a retained\nnative study. Defaults are its first eight panel positions, without selection.\nRequires an existing CUDA-enabled PyTorch/FlashInfer installation; installs\nnothing. This is a mechanism diagnostic, not a benchmark or significance test.\n\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\nDEFAULT_SEEDS = (\n    8159928092523588743, 8177797019031468917,\n    8391285739328190392, 8053119387325876827,\n    8196634704829835813, 8061472613692124596,\n    8340101673466357085, 7799700727755397900,\n)\nREVIEWED_WRAPPER_SHA256 = \"fca8a2ba3ef29ef0e6576f560af71257edf71473f9dcd1cd4d933fd3e20f4da1\"\n\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--seed\", action=\"append\", type=int,\n                        help=\"Repeat for each seed; defaults to eight retained panel positions\")\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    seeds = args.seed if args.seed is not None else list(DEFAULT_SEEDS)\n    if not seeds or any(s < 0 or s >= 2**63 for s in seeds):\n        parser.error(\"Provide at least one seed in [0, 2**63)\")\n    os.environ.setdefault(\"FLASHINFER_JIT_DEBUG\", \"0\")\n    import torch\n    import flashinfer\n    from flashinfer import sampling\n\n    if not torch.cuda.is_available():\n        raise RuntimeError(\"This reproducer requires a CUDA GPU; no CPU fallback\")\n    device = torch.device(\"cuda\", torch.cuda.current_device())\n    wrapper_sha = hashlib.sha256(Path(sampling.__file__).read_bytes()).hexdigest()\n    print(json.dumps({\"torch\": torch.__version__, \"torch_cuda\": torch.version.cuda,\n                      \"flashinfer\": flashinfer.__version__,\n                      \"gpu\": torch.cuda.get_device_name(device),\n                      \"wrapper_sha256\": wrapper_sha,\n                      \"matches_reviewed_wrapper\": wrapper_sha == REVIEWED_WRAPPER_SHA256,\n                      \"jit_debug\": os.environ[\"FLASHINFER_JIT_DEBUG\"]}))\n\n    # B=1, N=3, V=3, float32; the proposal really is the point mass at token 0.\n    q = torch.tensor([[[1., 0., 0.]] * 3], dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\n    draft_ids = torch.zeros((1, 3), dtype=torch.int32, device=device)\n    # Call 0 necessarily rejects its first proposal and returns token 1 or 2.\n    p0 = torch.tensor([[[0., .5, .5]] + [[.5, .5, 0.]] * 3],\n                      dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\n    # Call 1's first output is 0 exactly when its first proposal is accepted.\n    p1 = torch.tensor([[[.5, .5, 0.]] * 4], dtype=torch.float32, device=device)\n\n    def run_pair(seed, explicit_offsets=None):\n        generator = torch.Generator(device=device).manual_seed(seed)\n        calls = []\n        for j, target in enumerate((p0, p1)):\n            before = generator.get_offset()\n            kwargs = {\"generator\": generator} if explicit_offsets is None else {\n                \"seed\": seed, \"offset\": explicit_offsets[j]}\n            # The SAME private generator continues across the two managed calls.\n            y, accepted, emitted = sampling.chain_speculative_sampling(\n                q, draft_ids, target, deterministic=True, **kwargs)\n            torch.cuda.synchronize(device)\n            tokens = y[0].tolist()\n            a, e = int(accepted[0].item()), int(emitted[0].item())\n            if not (0 <= e <= a <= 3 and tokens[e+1:] == [-1] * (3-e)):\n                raise RuntimeError(\"Unexpected return/counter/padding contract\")\n            calls.append({\"tokens\": tokens, \"accepted\": a, \"emitted\": e,\n                          \"generator_offset_before\": before,\n                          \"generator_offset_after\": generator.get_offset(),\n                          \"explicit_offset\": None if explicit_offsets is None else explicit_offsets[j]})\n        if calls[0][\"emitted\"] != 0 or calls[0][\"tokens\"][0] not in (1, 2):\n            raise RuntimeError(\"The forced-rejection input did not return the expected support\")\n        if calls[1][\"tokens\"][0] not in (0, 1):\n            raise RuntimeError(\"The acceptance input did not return the expected support\")\n        bits = [int(calls[0][\"tokens\"][0] == 1), int(calls[1][\"tokens\"][0] == 0)]\n        return {\"calls\": calls, \"bits\": bits, \"E_bits_equal\": bits[0] == bits[1]}\n\n    counts = {\"managed\": 0, \"explicit_4_8\": 0, \"explicit_4_12\": 0}\n    for seed in seeds:\n        results = {\"managed\": run_pair(seed),\n                   \"explicit_4_8\": run_pair(seed, (4, 8)),\n                   \"explicit_4_12\": run_pair(seed, (4, 12))}\n        for mode, result in results.items():\n            counts[mode] += int(result[\"E_bits_equal\"])\n        def signature(result):\n            return [(c[\"tokens\"], c[\"accepted\"], c[\"emitted\"]) for c in result[\"calls\"]]\n        print(json.dumps({\"seed\": seed, \"results\": results,\n                          \"managed_matches_explicit_4_8_returns\":\n                              signature(results[\"managed\"]) == signature(results[\"explicit_4_8\"])}))\n    print(json.dumps({\"paired_positions\": len(seeds), \"E_counts\": counts,\n                      \"scope\": \"Two equal-shape calls only; no benchmark, p-value, or universal independence/fix claim\"}))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Error messages / logs\n\n```text\nNo exception or crash: this is an output-dependence/allocation issue.\nThe following are compact records extracted from the original native study, not stdout from the newly extracted standalone script.\nPanel positions are the first eight, unchanged and not selected by outcome.\n\nposition seed managed_bits explicit_4_8_bits explicit_4_12_bits\n0 8159928092523588743 [1, 1] [1, 1] [1, 1]\n1 8177797019031468917 [0, 0] [0, 0] [0, 1]\n2 8391285739328190392 [1, 1] [1, 1] [1, 1]\n3 8053119387325876827 [0, 0] [0, 0] [0, 1]\n4 8196634704829835813 [0, 0] [0, 0] [0, 1]\n5 8061472613692124596 [1, 1] [1, 1] [1, 0]\n6 8340101673466357085 [0, 0] [0, 0] [0, 1]\n7 7799700727755397900 [1, 1] [1, 1] [1, 0]\n\nExample of full retained returns, panel position 1:\noriginal: [{\"tokens\":[2,-1,-1,-1],\"accepted\":0,\"emitted\":0,\"launch_offset\":4,\"generator_offset_before\":0,\"generator_offset_after\":4},{\"tokens\":[1,-1,-1,-1],\"accepted\":0,\"emitted\":0,\"launch_offset\":8,\"generator_offset_before\":4,\"generator_offset_after\":8}]\nexplicit_overlap: [{\"tokens\":[2,-1,-1,-1],\"accepted\":0,\"emitted\":0,\"launch_offset\":4,\"generator_offset_before\":0,\"generator_offset_after\":0},{\"tokens\":[1,-1,-1,-1],\"accepted\":0,\"emitted\":0,\"launch_offset\":8,\"generator_offset_before\":0,\"generator_offset_after\":0}]\ndisjoint4: [{\"tokens\":[2,-1,-1,-1],\"accepted\":0,\"emitted\":0,\"launch_offset\":4,\"generator_offset_before\":0,\"generator_offset_after\":0},{\"tokens\":[0,1,-1,-1],\"accepted\":2,\"emitted\":1,\"launch_offset\":12,\"generator_offset_before\":0,\"generator_offset_after\":0}]\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\n### Revision and scope\n\nA source-only check on 2026-09-28 found `flashinfer/sampling.py` and `include/flashinfer/sampling.cuh` at main commit `85bfa5e4b599a9c616f073e2c06d3b9a1cdadee4` byte-identical to the tested pin. This is not a new runtime reproduction on main.\n\nReviewed file SHA-256:\n- wrapper: `fca8a2ba3ef29ef0e6576f560af71257edf71473f9dcd1cd4d933fd3e20f4da1`\n- kernel header: `e6288bbecabc433be8782d4e5fd9c7f9e3ba3815ddcde90176a750455a5daeba`\n\nThe complete retained study also used a fully specified four-token consumer with mathematical target mass 1/16 on each string in {1,2} x {0,1}^3. Its first-pair event E was (1,0) or (2,1), with mathematical target probability 1/2. The primary managed-versus-same-start-disjoint comparison had 111 versus 0 discordances and adjusted exact p=9.244463733058732e-33 within the original 12-test family. This is an existing result, not a test performed by the small script.\n\nAll five retained consumer arms had E counts 208, 208, 97, 106, 106 (N=208 each: original, overlap replay, Disjoint4, Disjoint0, target-only). Disjoint0 and target-only shared the first two tokens at all 208 positions, so their equal 106 counts are not independent confirmations; their full four-token outputs matched only 61/208. A generic legal input also detected original-mode dependence in the separate input comparison. No superiority claim follows from the crafted inputs.\n\nThis report does not establish language-model quality degradation, a universal API contract violation, or a generally correct fix. Explicit starts 4 and 12 isolate the overlap for the two equal-shape calls. Merely changing an increment to N+2 should not be treated as a universal repair: with advance-before-return semantics, unequal successive call shapes require separate reservation analysis and regression tests.\n\nRelated work in this repository: [#4861](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/4861) concerns per-request seed/offset indexing; [#5201](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/5201) concerns residual reduction while preserving RNG order. [#2345](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/pull/2345) concerns broader per-request generator support; its speculative-sampling evaluation comment is related context, not evidence attributing that regression to this overlap. These are other contributors' PRs, not fixes submitted with this report.\n\nThe original raw export remains local, SHA-256 `0deb6022e9ce3bb291877bf45d2d60bb227b92e69dd4c924636d43ce8bc97129`. No public full-artifact URL is asserted. The code, concrete retained examples and source links in this issue are the public report. No new GPU experiment was run to prepare it.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5637/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5637/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4731","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4731/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4731/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4731/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4731","id":5250982797,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABOPujjQ","number":4731,"title":"[sm120] NVFP4 KV: masked multi-token XQA verify (q_len_per_req>1) is intermittently ~3x slower, cancelling the MTP speedup; q_len=1 is fine","user":{"login":"ssubbotin","id":3616914,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2MTY5MTQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3616914?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin","html_url":"https://github.com/ssubbotin","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/ssubbotin/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":5761844621,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABV27FjQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/bug","name":"bug","color":"d73a4a","default":true,"description":"Something isn't working","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":10085239696,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACWSCLkA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/model:%20qwen3.5%20/%203.6%20/%203.8","name":"model: qwen3.5 / 3.6 / 3.8","color":"a8c6ea","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":10086441400,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACWTLhuA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/op:%20attention","name":"op: attention","color":"cd9a86","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":4,"created_at":"2026-08-25T21:44:40Z","updated_at":"2026-09-26T08:10:55Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Summary\n\nOn sm_120 (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell), `xqa_batch_decode_with_kv_cache` with **NVFP4 KV** (`kv_cache_sf` provided) behaves correctly and at normal speed for `q_len_per_req=1`, but with masked multi-token speculative verify (`q_len_per_req=4` + `mask` + `q_cu_seq_lens` from MTP k=3) draft acceptance intermittently collapses: final outputs stay correct (rejection sampling), yet the accepted-token rate drops enough to roughly double the number of decode steps, with strongly bimodal end-to-end decode speed (35–107 tok/s across otherwise identical runs; the FA2 route on the same stack gives a stable ~104).\n\nBecause `q_len_per_req=1` is healthy and the FA2 path with the same cache contents is healthy at all q lengths, the suspect is the masked multi-query fp4 path (verify logits for later in-block positions), rather than cache layout or scale handling.\n\n## Environment\n\n- flashinfer-python / flashinfer-cubin **0.6.16.post3**, torch 2.13.0+cu130, CUDA 13.0, sm_120 (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation, 96 GB), native Linux.\n- Integration: vLLM v0.27.2-era tree with vllm-project/vllm#46329 applied (NVFP4 KV on sm12x, HND layout, linear per-block V scale factors), model Qwen3.8-27B hybrid (16 full-attention layers, GQA 24/4, head_dim 256), MTP `num_speculative_tokens=3`, 262k context.\n\n## Call pattern\n\nPer decode step (uniform batch, CUDA-graph captured):\n\n```python\nxqa_batch_decode_with_kv_cache(\n    query=q_bf16,                        # [batch*4, 24, 256]\n    kv_cache=(k_data_u8, v_data_u8),     # HND, [pages, 4, page, 128] packed fp4\n    kv_cache_sf=(k_sf, v_sf),            # linear per-block SF views (strided)\n    workspace_buffer=...,\n    block_tables=..., seq_lens=..., max_seq_len=...,\n    bmm1_scale=softmax_scale * k_scale, bmm2_scale=v_scale,\n    kv_layout=\"HND\",\n    q_len_per_req=4,\n    mask=draft_block_mask,\n    q_cu_seq_lens=cu_lens,\n)\n```\n\n## Observations\n\n| configuration | result |\n|---|---|\n| q_len_per_req=1 (no spec decode), nvfp4 | correct, ~63–66 tok/s ≈ raw forward rate |\n| q_len_per_req=4 + mask, **fp8** KV, same stack | stable, acceptance 0.494/pos, ~110 tok/s |\n| q_len_per_req=4 + mask, **nvfp4** KV | correct outputs, but acceptance collapses intermittently; decode steps ~2x; bimodal 35–107 tok/s |\n| same nvfp4 cache read via FA2 paged prefill route | stable, acceptance 0.459/pos, ~104 tok/s |\n\nNeedle-in-haystack through the nvfp4 XQA path is 8/8 at ~24.5k and ~257k tokens (temperature 0), so plain retrieval/reads are fine; the degradation is specific to the masked multi-token verify scores.\n\n## Ask\n\nIs the masked multi-query path expected to support fp4 KV on sm_120, and if so, does the mask/e4m3-scale handling differ from the q=1 path in a way that could perturb later-position logits? Happy to build a standalone reproducer (fixed cache contents, q=4 masked call vs 4 sequential q=1 calls, compare logits) if that is the most useful next step — guidance on the expected tolerances for that comparison would help.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4731/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/4731/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5479","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5479/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5479/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5479/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5479","id":5548115085,"node_id":"I_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABSrGEjQ","number":5479,"title":"[Bug ]err in compilation code about \"Unknown option --compress-mode=size \" when using FlashInfer version 0.6.18, which is automatically downloaded by vLLM 0.29.0.","user":{"login":"griezswift","id":103055835,"node_id":"U_kgDOBiSB2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/103055835?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift","html_url":"https://github.com/griezswift","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/griezswift/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":5761844621,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAABV27FjQ","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/bug","name":"bug","color":"d73a4a","default":true,"description":"Something isn't working","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":9345322166,"node_id":"LA_kwDOJ-UyCM8AAAACLQZMtg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/labels/needs-triage","name":"needs-triage","color":"f9d0c4","default":false,"description":"","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"apex-mochen","id":265981728,"node_id":"U_kgDOD9qPIA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/265981728?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen","html_url":"https://github.com/apex-mochen","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":2,"created_at":"2026-09-23T03:01:45Z","updated_at":"2026-09-23T15:02:53Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"apex-mochen","id":265981728,"node_id":"U_kgDOD9qPIA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/265981728?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen","html_url":"https://github.com/apex-mochen","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/apex-mochen/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Before submitting\n\n- [x] I have searched existing issues and did not find a solution or an existing report.\n\n### Environment\n\n```text\n==== FlashInfer ====\nflashinfer                   : 0.6.18\nflashinfer commit            : 69ff11fc4954396d98326656dc85debd2223f637\nflashinfer file              : /home/xxx/test/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/__init__.py\nflashinfer-cubin             : not installed\nflashinfer-jit-cache         : not installed\nJIT cache                    : /home/xxx/.cache/flashinfer (1 compiled .so)\nCubin store (local)          : /home/xxx/.cache/flashinfer/cubins (0 cubins, 0 MiB)\nTarget CUDA archs (resolved) : {(8, '6')}\n\n==== Python / Platform ====\nPython            : 3.12.14 (main, Sep  1 2026, 14:16:52) [Clang 22.1.3 ]\nPython executable : /home/xxx/test/.venv/bin/python\nVirtual env       : /home/xxx/test/.venv\nPlatform          : Linux-6.5.0-18-generic-x86_64-with-glibc2.35\nlibc              : glibc 2.35\nOS                : Ubuntu 22.04.4 LTS\nContainer         : no / not detected\n\n==== GPU / Driver ====\nDriver version       : 595.91.07\nCUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES : <unset>\nGPU 0 (CUDA order)   : NVIDIA RTX A6000 | SM86 | 84 SMs | 47.4 GiB\nGPU 1 (CUDA order)   : NVIDIA RTX A6000 | SM86 | 84 SMs | 47.4 GiB\nGPU 2 (CUDA order)   : NVIDIA RTX A6000 | SM86 | 84 SMs | 47.4 GiB\nGPU 3 (CUDA order)   : NVIDIA RTX A6000 | SM86 | 84 SMs | 47.4 GiB\nGPU 4 (CUDA order)   : NVIDIA RTX A6000 | SM86 | 84 SMs | 47.4 GiB\nGPU 5 (CUDA order)   : NVIDIA RTX A6000 | SM86 | 84 SMs | 47.4 GiB\nGPU 6 (CUDA order)   : NVIDIA RTX A6000 | SM86 | 84 SMs | 47.4 GiB\nGPU 7 (CUDA order)   : NVIDIA RTX A6000 | SM86 | 84 SMs | 47.4 GiB\n\n==== CUDA Toolkit ====\nnvcc on PATH                            : not found\nCUDA_HOME (env)                         : <unset>\nCUDA toolkit resolved by flashinfer JIT : /usr/local/cuda (version 12.1)\n\n==== PyTorch ====\ntorch                          : 2.13.0+cu130\ntorch.version.cuda             : 13.0\ntorch CXX11 ABI                : True\ntorch compiled archs           : sm_75 sm_80 sm_86 sm_90 sm_100 sm_120\ntorch.backends.cudnn.version() : 92000\ntorch file                     : /home/xxx/test/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/__init__.py\n\n==== Relevant Packages ====\napache-tvm-ffi               : 0.1.11\ncuda-bindings                : 13.4.2\ncuda-core                    : 1.2.0\ncuda-pathfinder              : 1.8.2\ncuda-python                  : 13.4.1\ncuda-tile                    : 1.6.0\ncuda-toolkit                 : 13.0.3.0\nflashinfer-python            : 0.6.18\nninja                        : 1.13.2\nnumpy                        : 2.3.5\nnvidia-cublas                : 13.1.1.3\nnvidia-cuda-cccl             : 13.3.4.3.1\nnvidia-cuda-crt              : 13.4.92\nnvidia-cuda-cupti            : 13.0.85\nnvidia-cuda-nvcc             : 13.4.92\nnvidia-cuda-nvdisasm         : 13.4.92\nnvidia-cuda-nvrtc            : 13.0.88\nnvidia-cuda-runtime          : 13.0.96\nnvidia-cudnn-cu13            : 9.20.0.48\nnvidia-cudnn-frontend        : 1.29.0\nnvidia-cufft                 : 12.0.0.61\nnvidia-cufile                : 1.15.1.6\nnvidia-curand                : 10.4.0.35\nnvidia-cusolver              : 12.0.4.66\nnvidia-cusparse              : 12.6.3.3\nnvidia-cusparselt-cu13       : 0.8.1\nnvidia-cutlass-dsl           : 4.6.2\nnvidia-cutlass-dsl-libs-base : 4.6.2\nnvidia-cutlass-dsl-libs-core : 4.6.2\nnvidia-cutlass-dsl-libs-cu12 : 4.6.2\nnvidia-cutlass-dsl-libs-cu13 : 4.6.2\nnvidia-ml-py                 : 13.610.43\nnvidia-nccl-cu13             : 2.29.7\nnvidia-nvjitlink             : 13.4.92\nnvidia-nvshmem-cu13          : 3.4.5\nnvidia-nvtx                  : 13.0.85\nnvidia-nvvm                  : 13.4.92\ntorch                        : 2.13.0+cu130\ntorch-c-dlpack-ext           : 0.1.5\ntorchaudio                   : 2.11.0+cu130\ntorchcodec                   : 0.16.0+cu130\ntorchvision                  : 0.28.0+cu130\ntransformers                 : 5.17.0\ntriton                       : 3.7.1\nvllm                         : 0.29.0  (declares flashinfer-python==0.6.18)\n```\n\n### Bug description\n\nWhen attempting to launch Qwen3 series models (Qwen3-8B, Qwen3.5-35B-A3B, Qwen3.6-35B-A3B) using the `vllm serve` command within a fresh uv Python environment—following the instructions in the official documentation's \"Quickstart\" section—the service failed to start due to an error (as shown in the image below): the automatically installed flashinfer 0.6.18 failed to correctly compile code for certain CUDA kernels.\ninstalled instruction:\n```\nuv venv --python 3.12 --seed\nsource .venv/bin/activate\nuv pip install vllm --torch-backend=auto\n```\nvllm serve instruction:\n```\nvllm serve  /data/models/Qwen3.5-35B-A3B \\\n  --served-model-name Qwen3.5-35B-A3B \\\n  --host 0.0.0.0 \\\n  --port 8000 \\\n  --tensor-parallel-size 4 \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.8 \\\n  --max-model-len 16384 \\\n  --max-num-seqs 64 \\\n  --enable-prefix-caching \\\n  --enable-expert-parallel \\\n  --reasoning-parser qwen3 \\\n  --speculative-config '{\"method\":\"mtp\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":3}' \n```\nshowing error:\n> nvcc fatal : Unknown option '--compress-mode=size'\n\n\n### Minimal reproduction\n\n```python\nThe latest stable version of vLLM is currently 0.29.0; using version 0.28.0 or 0.30.0 might not allow for reproduction, so you need to specifically download version 0.29.0.\n\nuv venv --python 3.12 --seed\nsource .venv/bin/activate\nuv pip install vllm --torch-backend=auto\n```\n\n### Error messages / logs\n\n```text\nlike this:\n\n(EngineCore pid=3164713) ERROR 09-23 10:35:19 [core.py:1374] RuntimeError: Ninja build failed. Ninja output:\n(EngineCore pid=3164713) ERROR 09-23 10:35:19 [core.py:1374] ninja: Entering directory `/home/xxx/.cache/flashinfer/0.6.18/86/cached_ops/sampling'\n\nFAILED: [code=1] /home/xxx/.cache/flashinfer/0.6.18/86/cached_ops/sampling/csrc_renorm.cuda.o\n            (EngineCore pid=3164713) ERROR 09-23 10:35:19 [core.py:1374]  /usr/local/cuda/bin/nvcc --generate-dependencies-with-compile -MF /home/xxx/.cache/flashinfer/0.6.18/86/cached_ops/sampling/csrc_renorm.cuda.o.d -DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000 -D_GLIBCXX_USE_CXX11_ABI=1 -I/home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/cccl/cub -I/home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/cccl/libcudacxx/include -I/home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/cccl/thrust -isystem /home/xxx/.local/share/uv/python/cpython-3.12.14-linux-x86_64-gnu/include/python3.12 -isystem /usr/local/cuda/include -isystem /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/tvm_ffi/include -isystem /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/tvm_ffi/include -isystem /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/include -isystem /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/csrc -isystem /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/cutlass/include -isystem /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/cutlass/tools/util/include -isystem /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/spdlog/include --compiler-options=-fPIC --expt-relaxed-constexpr -gencode=arch=compute_86,code=sm_86 -DFLASHINFER_ENABLE_FP8_E8M0 -DFLASHINFER_ENABLE_FP4_E2M1 -std=c++17 --threads=1 -use_fast_math -Xfatbin=-compress-all --compress-mode=size -DFLASHINFER_ENABLE_F16 -DFLASHINFER_ENABLE_BF16 -DFLASHINFER_ENABLE_FP8_E4M3 -DFLASHINFER_ENABLE_FP8_E5M2 -DNDEBUG -O3 -c /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/data/csrc/renorm.cu -o /home/xxx/.cache/flashinfer/0.6.18/86/cached_ops/sampling/csrc_renorm.cuda.o\n\n(EngineCore pid=3164713) nvcc fatal   : Unknown option '--compress-mode=size'\n(EngineCore pid=3164713) ninja: build stopped: subcommand failed.\n(EngineCore pid=3164713)\n```\n\n### Additional context\n\nWhen I install vLLM 0.28.0, the imported flashinfer is as follows:\n```\n==== FlashInfer ====\nflashinfer                   : 0.6.16.post3\nflashinfer commit            : ?: AttributeError: module 'flashinfer' has no attribute '__git_commit__'\nflashinfer file              : /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/flashinfer/__init__.py\nflashinfer-cubin             : not installed\nflashinfer-jit-cache         : not installed\nJIT cache                    : /home/xxx/.cache/flashinfer (1 compiled .so)\nCubin store (local)          : /home/xxx/.cache/flashinfer/cubins (0 cubins, 0 MiB)\nTarget CUDA archs (resolved) : {(8, '6')}\n\n==== CUDA Toolkit ====\nnvcc on PATH                            : not found\nCUDA_HOME (env)                         : <unset>\nCUDA toolkit resolved by flashinfer JIT : /usr/local/cuda (version 12.1)\n\n==== PyTorch ====\ntorch                          : 2.13.0+cu132\ntorch.version.cuda             : 13.2\ntorch CXX11 ABI                : True\ntorch compiled archs           : sm_75 sm_80 sm_86 sm_90 sm_100 sm_120\ntorch.backends.cudnn.version() : 92000\ntorch file                     : /home/xxx/xxxenv/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/__init__.py\n```\nAt this point, the vllm serve service can start up normally.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5479/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5479/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5445","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5445/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5445/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer/issues/5445/events","html_url":"https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/